28-year-old chief was found dead in a shopping mall in San Francisco
The 28-year-old chef, who had recently moved to San Francisco, was found dead in the stairwell of a local shopping mall this week.
However, the victim’s brother says he doesn’t know anyone who wanted to hurt him and says, ‘His business finally went well.’
The body, which was found at the Westfield Mall on Wednesday morning, was identified as 28-year-old Frank Galicia of San Francisco.
The San Francisco Police Department said the death was defined as a homicide and that an investigation is ongoing.
The victim's brother, Louis Galicia, told ABC's channel KGO in San Francisco that Frank, who had previously worked as a line chef in Boston, had had his dream job as a line chef at the San Francisco restaurant Sons & Daughters six months ago.
Sons & Daughters said they were “horrified and shocked” because of his death.
“We have a small team that works like a close-knit family, and they’re going to miss him very much,” the spokesman said.
We send our thoughts and condolences to Frank's family and friends during this awkward time.
Louis Galicia said Frank spent the first night in hostels, but that recently “his business was finally going well.”
‘He found an apartment and dated a girl,’ Louis Galicia told KGO.
Louis Galicia said he doesn't know anyone who wanted to hurt his younger brother.
He was benevolent and had a big heart.
“His way of feeling connected with the family was to cook for us, to have dinner,” Louis Galicia said.
He never wanted to be involved in any kind of controversy.
He was the brother who drifted with the flow.
‘In a world where so many things are wrong, he was an unpolished diamond that shone brighter every day,’ he said.
Everyone who has knowledge of the matter is asked to call the San Francisco Police Department hotline at 415/575-4444.
Doctor trainees on strike: Demands for new industrial action
Representatives of medical trainees have asked their trade union to take new industrial action in their dispute regarding the new agreement.
The Junior Doctors Committee (JDC) of the British Medical Association (BMA) asks the entire Council to support new industrial action from the beginning of September.
The JDC says the ministers have failed to address concerns about the agreement.
Medical trainees and medical students voted in July to reject the agreement that had been agreed with the BMA.
58% of the voters rejected it.
In a letter posted on Twitter on Thursday night, Ellen McCourt, chairman of the JDC (JDC), said the board had remained ‘stubbornly tacit’ issues that she said led to the rejection of the agreement.
He said: “In this light, the JDC’s government has voted to completely reject the proposed new agreement and call for formal renegotiations on all your concerns.”
In response to the government's silence, JDC's Board of Directors has today submitted a formal request for a special meeting of the BMA Council in order to be empowered for the gradually advancing and expanding industrial action programme, which will begin in early September.
The controversy has resulted that medical trainees have participated in six strikes this year, including the first complete work stoppage in the history of the NHS healthcare system.
The head of BMA's medical interns, Dr. Johann Malawana, resigned after rejecting the terms negotiated with the agreement, the BMA's recommendations.
He had told BMA members at the meetings prior to 54 000 doctor trainees and students voting that the agreement was good and should be approved.
After the vote, Health Secretary Jeremy Hunt said the agreement would be imposed on doctors in England.
Russia and Turkey: an alliance of outsiders?
A gesture that ended in a crisis.
On Tuesday, the leaders of Russia and Turkey met to shake hands with each other and officially declare the eight-month battle between words and economic sanctions.
But when Vladimir Putin greeted his Turkish colleague in the gold-plated hall of St. Peter's Palace, I got the clear impression that Ankara wanted more of this reconciliation.
Yes, we shook hands at the event.
But Putin's smile looked forced, and he didn't exude much heat, even according to his own restrained standards.
Recep Tayyip Erdogan, on the other hand, constantly talked about his “dear friend” Putin – five times according to a report.
I went crazy in the calculations.
He also swore that relations with Russia would return not only to the level before the crisis, but even better.
The next day, the newspapers here described Erdogan's behavior as if nothing bad had ever happened.
In my opinion, his zeality reflected the opposite.
But the coolness radiant from Putin showed that the Russian leader hasn't forgotten anything.
In fact, the cause of the crisis was the first thing he mentioned in his opening speech: when Turkey shot down a Russian combat aircraft on the Syrian border.
Moscow's reaction at the time was outrageous.
Putin invaded Ankara for stabbing Moscow in the back.
The insult was even worse when it came from an supposed friend.
Rebuilding true trust will be difficult, perhaps impossible.
Russia's public opinion has also changed since November.
For months, state-ruled media led an astounding, thorough attack on Ankara.
All of a sudden it seemed that the Turks were to blame for everything.
Among the most serious were the accusations made by senior officials that Erdogan's own family has benefited from illegal oil trade from ISIS-controlled Syrian territories.
He has strongly denied this.
But in St. Petersburg the official message was that it's time to move on.
At the end of the day, this meeting took place only because Putin received the apologies he demanded from President Erdogan.
Russia has achieved some kind of victory.
The benefits of agreeinging a truce are clear.
First of all, Erdogan needs all possible friends after he was almost ousted from power in last month's failed coup.
The ongoing terrorist attacks in Turkey have also clearly shook him.
In addition, there is an economic motive.
Russia's economic sanctions have been burdensome, in particular the ban on charter flights, which usually carry several million Russian tourists to the Turkish coast each year.
The number has decreased by almost 90%.
With regard to Russia, local tour operators and charter airlines are certainly relieved when the flights eventually continue.
They're hoping for a late-season rush into the Mediterranean.
And already this week, state TV has been predicting cheaper fruits and vegetables since the import of agricultural products from Turkey is again allowed.
‘Tourists there, tomatoes back here,’ as formulated in Vedemosti newspaper’s report.
But the visit also had political added value for Moscow.
Ankara is angry at the West about what is considered a weak response to an attempted coup.
When a long-standing resentment is added to the snail's fast-paced EU membership negotiations, Putin emerges, eager to take advantage of coolness and erode Turkey's ties with the West.
Russia's leader certainly received loose points from Ankara, where he called after the revolutionary attempt to support the designated authorities.
Of course, this is obvious to Moscow, who has his own deep-rooted fear of a change of power.
So, this meeting at the blazing seaside palace made it possible for Russia and Turkey to introduce an ‘consortium of outsiders’, as an analyst described the situation to me: two countries that feel that the West abandoned them and mistreated them, uniting their forces.
However, these two have major disagreements, despite public reconciliation.
The key problem is Syria, in which Moscow has recently reconciled itself to the role of a peacemaker, but in which Russia and Turkey support the opposing sides.
It could be a lot that after almost three hours of preliminary talks, the presidents told the press that they hadn't even dealt with it.
The Turkish president deliberately avoided answering a question about their disagreements, while Putin chose to emphasize them.
There is no clear consensus on where they can seek a common basis for Syria.
After months of open hostility, and given the possibility of a full disaster when NATO member Turkey fires down a Russian combat aircraft, it is certainly better that the two leaders are even on speaking terms again.
Royal Bank of Scotland disappears from non-Scotland customers
According to the Bank's CEO, the RBS brand will be reduced to a back office role.
Royal Bank of Scotland disappears from non-Scotland customers.
Ross McEwan told BBC Scotland that the RBS brand was linked to the bank's international goals.
Since the bank almost collapsed eight years ago and had to be saved, it has given up these goals.
At this time, the bank’s strategists have used the ‘RBS’ brand to protect other consumer finance brands.
It was backed by millions of pounds used to sponsor international sports, from the Six Nations rugby to Wimbledon champion Andy Murray.
But now it has been considered right to let more national brands come to the forefront.
The Royal Bank of Scotland is in use with Scottish customers, but it won't start again from scratch.
In England and Wales, with the exception of headquarters and stock exchange listings, all RBS references will be converted into NatWest.
The Ulster Bank brand is already in use by customers in Ireland and Northern Ireland.
There are other, smaller private banking brands that get more visibility: Coutts, Adam & Co, Drummond and Holt’s Military Bank.
McEwan was interviewed on a tour of customers and staff in Inverness-shire.
He told BBC Scotland: ‘The RBS will become the employer of our investor brand and employees, because we are now becoming much more of a bank of brands.”
When the bank itself became a global brand, RBS became a global brand.
So I say that we no longer have global goals, we have local goals.
Each of these brands means something very different in their own communities, and our staff works with customers under these brands.
RBS has already stated that it will not continue to sponsor the Six Nations, and it has raised the profile of different brands in its sports sponsorship.
“It’s now time to move towards a bank of brands, because fundamentally (we’ve been asking) it’s a question of how we can focus on making it a better bank for our customers,” the CEO said.
“Three years ago, I would have been very cynical if we had said that we were going to be a great bank for our customers and offered these brands.
But with the work we've done focused on customer needs, not ourselves, I think you'll see a lot of changes.
We can bring those brands back, so I think that's the right time.
Frankie Dettori wins at Newmarket with Predilection and achieves 3000 wins
Dettori sprays champagne after having a total of 3000 victories after a double win
Racher Frankie Dettori secured his 3000th British victory at Newmarket on Friday.
He reached the milestone with his second victory of the night on his home track while competing on a Predilection coached by John Gosden.
The 45-year-old Italian is the sixth jockey to reach this milestone in racing.
He's following Sir Gordon Richards, Doug Smith, Lester Piggott, Pat Eddery and Willie Carson.
The three-time master jockey Dettor, who previously won at Ghayyari, said: “This is special.”
I'm very emotional because my family's here too.
I'm very happy that this happened in Newmarket, because here I started 30 years ago.
It's not a big event, it's not the Royal Ascot, it's an ordinary Friday night and 20 000 people.
I'm relieved and very humble.
The dettor absorbs cheers as he leads Predilection on display after the victory.
Two rivers have reached this milestone in the steeplechase races - master jockey Richard Johnson and retired 20-time champion Sir Anthony McCoy, who had more than 4300 victories in his record-breaking career.
Lester Piggot, 9-time Derby winner, 4493 wins
Frankie is one of the best rivers of the current era, and it's no surprise that he's reached this milestone that only a few have accomplished before him.
He's stylish and strong, but his tactical intelligence separates him from others in the weighing room.
He is a great ambassador for competitions, his enthusiasm and charisma have attracted fans to the sport for years.
A few weeks ago, I told Frankie in Epsom that he would've rided 6000 winners if he wasn't just a weekend and a big-race jockey!
Seriously, Frankie has ridden the winners in Great Britain for most of the 30 years, which is great to see.
He's a lively person who's a good thing for British competition, but he's also an incredible jockey.
Frankie Dettori celebrated the milestone with a flying landing from the saddle.
From voluntary cessations to high-flying landings, Frankie Dettori has been the face of British racing for two decades.
It's been a rollercoaster.
A heap of victories in a big race, including his famous ‘Magnificent Seven’ victory in Ascot in 1996, but he also survived a plane crash and was banned from competition because of drugs.
Three years ago, he initially struggled to return after a six-month ban, but he showed a significant strength of character in challenging the skeptics and returning in his best condition.
And he may continue for a long time - Dettori has previously said that he would like to continue to compete at least until the age of 50.
Russia's Putin dismisses Chief of Staff Sergei Ivanov
Russia's president, Vladimir Putin, unexpectedly expelled Chief of Staff Sergei Ivanov.
Ivanov has been part of Putin's trusted insider for many years.
The 63-year-old has now been appointed Special Representative for the Environment and Transport.
The Kremlin's statement said that Putin "has ordered Ivanov to step away from the duties of the leader of the Russian presidential regime," but there was no reason.
Ivanov's Deputy Director since 2012, Anton Vaino, has been appointed as his successor.
Persecution, 44, is a former diplomat.
Born in Tallinn, Estonia in 2012, graduated from the prestigious Moscow State Institute of International Relations (MGIMO) and served at the Embassy of Tokyo.
Later, he led the president's protocol and board staff, according to the Kremlin's website (in Russian).
When he was appointed, he said to Putin, “Thank you for your confidence.”
In my opinion, the most important task of the administration is to support your activities as Head of State when it comes to drafting laws and managing the implementation of your guidelines.
Putin told a Russian TV station on Friday that Ivanov had asked to be allowed to leave his position and recommended that Vaino be appointed to replace him.
Are the smiles convincing?
It's a movement that's confused Moscow.
Sergei Ivanov has long been one of Vladimir Putin's closest allies and, like Putin, served on the KGB.
As Chief of Staff, he was one of the country's most influential men.
In a meeting with President Putin on state television, both men argued that the Chief of Staff withdrew at his own request.
But in spite of the smile on the cameras, only a few here are convinced - especially now, just before the parliamentary elections.
Is this just the aftermath of some kind of power struggle?
No one knows yet.
But the official claim that a man who was once advertised as a potential presidential nominee suddenly wants to lead Russia's environmental policy has been very skeptical.
In his comments quoted on the Kremlin’s website, Ivanov said “it’s true that early in 2012 I asked you in a conversation to give me this very complex task for four years.
Well, now I’ve been Chief of Staff for four years and eight months.”
Ivanov approved the position in December 2011.
He previously served as Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Defence.
He is a member of the Russian Security Council and a former member of the KGB, as well as Putin.
In the late 1990's, when Putin was the leader of the FSB, which replaced the KGB, Ivanov was appointed as his vice president.
When Putin came to power, he mentioned Ivanov as one of the five people he trusted the most.
It was once thought that Ivanov could become Russian President Putin after the second term, as Putin's third term would have been unconstitutional.
However, this mission went to another close ally of Putin, Dmitry Medvedev.
Putin became prime minister before his return to president just three and a half years later.
Criminal Minds star Thomas Gibson was fired after hitting the producer
Gibson was involved in a physical controversy last month on the set in Los Angeles.
Producers of the series, ABC Studios and CBS Television Studios, announced his expulsion in a joint statement.
Gibson, who played Special Agent Aaron Hotchner, issued an apology on Thursday before his dismissal was announced.
There were creative differences and quarrels at the scene.
I regret that it happened,” he said in a statement.
According to a statement from ABC Studios and CBS Television Studios, details of how Gibson's character will be removed from the FBI agents' team can be expected later.
The 54-year-old has been involved in the series since its first season in 2005.
“I love the Criminal Minds series, and I’ve given it my all for the last twelve years,” Gibson said in a statement.
I had hoped I'd come all the way to the end, but it's not possible right now.
I just want to thank the writers, the producers, the actors, our incredible team and, above all, the best fans the series can have.
The twelfth season of the series is scheduled for September 28.
Mystery fish ended up in the garden in Banff
A man who lives in Banff has asked for help to identify the fish that have appeared in his garden.
Kevin Bain believes that 75 fish could be the swirls the vortex has left behind.
Bain, who lives 500 meters from the sea, published footage of the revelation in Periscope, hoping that someone could bring clarity to the appearance of two-inch fish.
Queensferry Crossing: The bridge now extends to Fifee
The 1.35 billion pound project is now expected to be completed by May 2017
Engineers have connected the northern deck of Queensferry's overpassage site as well as the ground bridge, which means that the bridge now extends to Fifee.
It is the first of the four locks between ‘deck fans’ that are now about to be completed around each of the three pillars of the bridge.
The bridge section of Fife is now 600 metres long and weighs 30 000 tonnes.
It has 10 000 tonnes of steel and 20 000 tonnes of concrete and 46 ropes.
The 1.35-billion-pound project is now expected to be completed by May 2017.
Economy Minister Keith Brown visited the construction site today and was among the first to walk from the ground to the bridge.
He said: “This is a historical and symbolic moment in the construction of Queensferry’s overpass.
We are all witnessing truly impressive engineering work in this project, where more than 30 000 tons of concrete and steel have been used alone for the construction of this bridge section.”
Despite the enormous size and weight of the bridge, finalizing the closure between the ground bridge and the bridge deck is a demanding operation in which the extremely precise dimensions must fit together.
Overall, almost 79% of the bridge deck is now in place, and the concrete lid for the last section of the lid is cast today in Rosyth, which means that the entire deck is ready to be lifted into place on the bridge.
Michael Martin, head of the Fort Crossing Bridge Constructors project, said: “The first shutdown in any bridge project is always a significant milestone.
In this fantastic project, this lock represents the tip of civil engineering.
When the last section of the road deck had been mounted and connected to the expansive road deck of the northern pole, we then had to close the remaining section on the side of the north, i.e. inland.
This was done by pulling the ground bridge of the northern fairway 700 millimetres in the direction of the south.
This was a huge and at the same time very demanding procedure.
Huge, because the ground bridge is 222 meters long, weighs about 6000 tons and had to be pulled at an angle of about 3%.
Demanding, because the permissible dimensional deviations according to which the team worked were tiny, just a few millimetres in both directions.
Fortunately, everything went well.
Saving Moelfre Bay: “Three men are ‘lucky to be alive’
Three men are ‘lucky to be alive’ after they fell from their fishing boat outside Anglesey.
The Moelfre RNLI rescue boat station sounded an alarm at 13:20 (BST) on Friday after the crew saw the event developing in Moelfre Bay.
Three men desperately tried to swam to safety as their boat moved on by themselves.
“We saw the boat moving closer to the men and we imagined the worst,” said Vince Jones.
‘Fortunately, a strong wind had blown it away from them by the time we got going,’ he added.
A local fishing boat rescued two of the men, and a third, 60-year-old man was found exhausted hanging from the buoy without a life jacket.
The men were taken to a coastal rescue boat and were brought back to the lifeboat station.
Jones said: “These three men are lucky to be alive and completely undamaged.
There was a kill switch rope in the boat's engine, but it had somehow detached from the person driving the boat when the boat capsized.
Someone took care of these guys today.
Pokémon Go is a respectable hunt for health and happiness
It's fascinating to see how the world changes.
Over the past decade, many have been worrying that teenagers and children no longer go out to play, but that they prefer to spend their free time watching screens.
Pokémon Go makes people get up from the couch and take part in some activity.
The screening time was originally limited to television, then video games, and now recently to smartphones.
So it's great to see that technology and outdoor activity aren't mutually exclusive.
Of course, I'm referring to Pokémon Go, an app based on characters that were originally developed in the 1990's for video games (later, they gave rise to a series of drawings, playing cards, and other sequels).
Like many phenomena of pop culture, its popularity was somewhat diluted before the release of this new game in the United States, Australia and New Zealand early in July.
After that, the release of the game in other countries was postponed, as huge popularity overloaded the servers.
In just days Pokémon Go had more users than Tinder and almost as much as Twitter.
Then how does it work?
Explained in a nutshell, players use their smartphones to search for Pokémon, which are traced by GPS, and which can be “photographed” (using the phone’s screenshot) or “catchery”.
A generation that played the game when they were younger has joined the craze and jumped up from the couch to the search.
In the United States, the game has led to a significant increase in the “average-average” daily steps.
The Apple Watch app Cardiogram, which tracks 35,000 users, recently told The Washington Post that within two days of the game's release, the number of people moving 30 minutes a day jumped from 45 percent to 53 percent of users tracked by Cardiogram.
The company doesn't know who's playing Pokémon Go, it only sees training level data.
Many public health promotions ‘discover 30’, ‘rise on the couch’ and ‘be active every day’ have not yielded any results because the majority of public health warnings are dull and mediated by people who seem unhappy.
However, the importance of exercise cannot be underestimated.
People who exercise regularly have a lower risk of cancer or depression.
Activity helps with weight management, high blood pressure and diabetes. Its benefits are too diversified to be listed here.
Pokémon Go's beauty is that it makes people go out and do something they enjoy.
This covers the three pillars of health: exercise, fresh air and sunshine, as well as fun.
Some people even make new friends when they hunt.
As usually, disapproving buzzards are looking here for the things to complain about.
Yes, someone can walk towards the door.
Well, then?
There's been a breakthrough.
Even though there used to be apps like Fitbit to monitor activity, they didn't give you a reason to move around the same way as this game.
Novelty charm disappears and use subsides in time.
However, due to Pokémon's success, there will be other games that combine applications and virtual reality with real-world activity, helping many hustling people to be more active, in better shape and healthier.
Read Dr. Joe's blog at www.drjoetoday.com
Simone Biles won gold in women's gymnastics multi-match
Forget the pressure.
Forget the publicity controversy.
Simone Biles is immune to it all.
Energetic in jumps.
Smooth on the boom.
Unbelievable on the floor.
Brilliant everywhere.
And now, finally, the Olympic champion.
The 19-year-old American gymnast won the multi-match title on Thursday, leaving the gap between himself and the rest of the world in the Olympic spotlight.
His total score, 62.198, was clearly better than silver medalist and “Final Five” teammate Aly Raisman and Russian bronze medallist Aliya Mustafina.
Simone Biles of the United States balance boom at the women's gymnastic multi-match final at the 2016 Summer Olympics in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil on Thursday.
Biles became the fourth American woman to win the multi-match championship in a row, and, in total, the fifth American champion. At the same time, she strengthened her reputation as the best of her generation, and perhaps all time.
He burst into tears when his final point was announced, and his long journey towards this moment ended.
This accomplishment raises him to the same class with the best athletes of his generation, such as Michael Phelps, who have raised their sport to the next level:
