The last test waited in Brazil, a race that was not so much between Biles and other rivals, but between him and the burden of excessive expectations.
Going back to the family's home in Spring, Texas, without a handful of gold medals would have been a disappointment.
Simone Biles of the United States, on the left, and Aly Raisman hug after winning a gold and silver medal (in this orderary) in a women's gymnastics multi-match final at the 2016 Summer Olympics in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil on Thursday.
Biles won his first medal on Tuesday when she culminated in the kick-off celebrations of Martha Karoly, the coordinator of the retiring national team.
Although Biles argued that he has never thought about the future as he went his long way to the top, it's not entirely truthful.
Part of his permanto program - which includes his trademark volt hit - is matched with Latin American music, which would also be perfect for the street next to the Rio Olympic arena.
This isn't a coincidence.
From this girl, adopted as a toddler by his grandparents, whom the mother of coach Aimee Boorman discovered when the girl was on a study trip to the gym where Boorman coached has become a powerhouse.
He hasn't lost in a multi-match since the summer of 2013, and this winning streak is likely to last as long as Biles wants.
Even though Mustafina was in a small lead after two rounds, it was just an illusion.
The balance boom and the patern in which Biles is the reigning world champion, expected.
He rose to the top with a boom point of 15.433 and crossed them by gaining 15.933 on the floor.
Raisman hugged him benevolently as they waited for the ending points - a mere formality - and the tears came to Biles' glitter red, white, and blue-limited eyes, when the dream from which he once wrote in his scrapbook became a reality.
Raisman's performance was some kind of rematch from four years ago, when he was in shared third place, but lost the bronze to Mustafina in the replay.
This time, the 22-year-old teammain, whom Americans call “grandmother,” was clearly in the lead.
He sent kisses of flights to the public after an emotional turmoil that sometimes seemed questionable.
As for Biles, this 19-year-old's widespread tweeting from this week will take on a whole new meaning:
Police: Three casualties in a serial killing were shot as they listened to music in the car
Two women and a 12-year-old girl who died in the deadliest attack of Phoenix's first serial killer of the last decade were sitting in the car chatting and listening to music when they were shot, according to a police bulletin released on Thursday.
Authorities said Angela Rochelle Liner, Stefanie R. Ellis and Ellis' daughter Maleah were shot in a car parked on the driveway on June 12.
According to a statement, the shooter on the passenger side of the car fired a handgun up to eight times and then fled another vehicle.
According to the police, the triple shooting did not appear to have been the result of the robbery, as the attacker did not take handbags or 2900 dollars to another adult victim.
Sergeant Jonathan Howard, a spokesman for the Phoenix Police Department, said the shooting prompted investigators to see if it was linked to four previous attacks.
A total of seven people have been killed and two have been wounded in nine attacks over a four-month period, carried out in two mainly Latin American districts.
“These things are taking place in a residential area in front of the home,” Howard said earlier this month to CBS subsidiary KPHO.
The suspect is believed to have used a number of vehicles, such as the brown Nissan of the late 1990s, the black BMW of the late 1990s, as well as white Cadillac or Lincoln.
Investigators have refused to reveal the evidence, which led to the conclusion that the attacks are interconnected and made by the same killer.
Ellis, 33, was hospitalized because of her injuries, where she later died.
Liner, 31, and Ellis's daughter, Maleah, died at the scene.
An eyewitness told the police that they were often sitting in a parked car listening to music.
Authorities also published a police bulletin about the shooting, which took place only half an hour before the triple shooting, targeting an emptied convertible van. This is also believed to have been the work of a serial killer.
Investigators found bullet casings, a bullet's seat and bullet fragments in the engine compartment.
The mother of a 6-year-old with microcephaly: ‘The situation will get easier’
Edmund Picciuto, like the majority of other six-year-olds, loves singing, toys and, of course, his mother.
But, unlike a large part of his peers, Edmund is a small-headed. This is the result of a genetic disorder that her mother, Elizabeth Picciuto, didn't know when she was pregnant.
“When he came home for the first time, my first thought was that I couldn’t do this.
I can't do this, okay?
I didn't agree to something like this.
“Which isn’t true at all – of course I promised,” he said.
She and her husband, Vincent, were told that Edmund may never learn to recognize them, and that she may not even survive.
Some doctors even suggested placing him in institutional care.
‘My first reaction was ‘I didn’t know people were still doing it,’” Picciuto said.
Edmund walks with the help of a walking rack.
Edmund didn't sit or crawl until he was three, and he still doesn't talk.
But he slowly reaches some milestones.
He walks assisted and rides a bicycle.
In addition, he loves to play with his two brothers and sing.
Elizabeth shows how Edmund’s invented signposts “would you sing to me.”
‘We’ll teach him sign language,’ Picciuto told CBS News.
But it's not the usual sign language - he says Edmund has come up with some of his own signs.
Microcephaly can be caused by a number of reasons: genetic, such as Edmund, or viral infections during pregnancy, such as measles or Zika virus.
Symptoms and prognosis can vary greatly.
It's too early to say how it will affect the lives of those infected with Zika.
Picciuto remembers the emotional load caused by an uncertain future.
I was just askinging what was going to happen.
What's going to happen?
And he still doesn't really know.
In fact, I haven't asked for a prognosis in a while, because I don't expect anyone to be able to actually give that to me.
But Picciuto has hopeful advice for mothers whose babies have a birth defect due to Zika.
It'll get easier.
You'll think it's going to get better.
You will love your child, and your child will love you.
Heavy rain and flood caused rescue operations in Missississippi, Louisiana
Heavy rain and widespread flooding in Louisiana prompted the governor to declare a state of emergency on Friday. More rain was expected in the state on Saturday.
The National Weather Service reports that numerous rivers in south-eastern Louisiana and the southern Mississippi were flooding across their edges and caused widespread flooding due to extremely heavy rains.
Louisiana's governor, John Bel Edwards, said state authorities are in constant contact with the local authorities, and that help is already coming to counties that need it.
Mike Steele, a spokeswoman for the governor's security and emergency preparedness office, said they received requests for high tide vehicles, boats and sand sacks.
Steele said Tangipahoa county alone asked for tens of thousands of sandbacks.
Until Saturday, the flood guard will run in a large part of the southern Louisiana.
The weather service said in a statement that another three to five centimetres of more rain is expected in the area.
In southern Alabama, a flood guard was in effect on Friday as the rain continued in the Mobile area.
The Comite River, which flows near Baton Rouge, as well as the Amite River near Denham Springs, are both located in Louisiana and were predicted to set a flood peak record over the weekend.
Weather forecaster Alek Krautmann said both rivers could be flooded into several houses in suburbs near Baton Rouge.
He also said there was a threat of flooding the Ascension county along the river below as flooded rivers slowly flow into Lake Maurepas.
The Tickfaw River, flowing south of Mississippi's state border in Lousiana's Liverpool, was at its all-time high on Friday morning at 9pm.
Rescuers are still picking people out of the floodwaters in Amite and Wilkinson counties in southwestern Mississippi.
Leroy Hansford and his wife and stepsole were among those rescued near Gloster on Friday.
The 62-year-old Hansford says the water at Beaver Creek, which is normally more than 400 feet away from his house, rose quickly during the night.
He says the other stepson, who lives nearby, warned him.
‘We woke up, and the water just kept climbing,’ Hansford said.
It stretched all the way to my waist.
Hansford's wife told her husband that she hasn't seen the creek's water so high during the 48 years she's lived.
Hansford said that both he and his family members all have injuries, and that he is the only one who is able to swim.
