A drug that costs two pounds a day can reduce the risk of a heart attack
Thousands of heart attack patients are benefitinging two pounds a day from a paiding drug after the authorities recommended that it be given to humans for longer.
The anti-coagulant drug ticagrelor reduces the risk of recurrent seizures in people with heart disease.
The medicine is given as early as 12 months after a heart attack in order to reduce the risk of a stroke or another seizure.
NICE, the NHS supervisor, has advised that it should be given to humans for a period of four years in order to further reduce the risk of cardiovascular problems.
But now NICE, the NHS supervisor, has advised that it should be given to humans for four years in order to further reduce the risk of cardiovascular problems.
Approximately 140 000 people suffer a heart attack in England every year, and a quarter of them have a second seizure or stroke.
Heart attacks and strokes are caused by fatty matter accumulated in the wall of the artery, which forms stenosis.
If stenosis ruptures, it can cause a blood clot, which prevents blood flow to the heart, causing a heart attack.
If a clot is released, it can travel with the blood and break the blood flow to the brain, causing a stroke.
Individuals who have already had one seizure have an increased risk of getting another.
Ticagrelor, produced by AstraZeneca in the UK, and sold under the trade name Brilique, lowers this risk by making coagulation less likely.
The NICE guidelines, published today, recommend that people be given 90 mg ticagrelor for 12 months, followed twice a day with aspirin 60 mg for the next two years.
Professor Carole Longson, director of NICE’s Health Technology Assessment Center, said: “Despite the availability of effective anticipating treatments, up to a quarter of people who have had a heart attack later have another heart attack or stroke, the consequences of which are often devastating.”
Fear of renewal can have a noticeable negative effect on a person's quality of life.
Evidence shows that ticagrelor in combination with aspirin is effective at reducing the risk of subsequent heart attacks and strokes in people who have already had a heart attack.
In our preliminary recommendation of ticagrelor, we are pleased to be able to add available treatment options to several thousand people who may benefit from them.
As data on the efficacy and safety of ticagrelor, in particular with regard to the risk of haemorrhage, are limited over three-year periods, no treatment is recommended in the draft guidelines for longer than during this period.
