Vinyl destination: who actually buys records?
Lonely, middle-aged men love vinyls.
Before you rush to fill in the comments section with irritated insults with the pseudonym ‘NotAloneousMediardMen, please note that this statement is based on actual information.
According to YouGov, the much-discussed new arrival of the albums is not due to the fact that more and more people born in this millennium get excited about the novelty of physical objects, but about the longings of middle age.
Apparently, those who have recently bought a vinyl record are most likely between the ages of 45 and 54.
In fact, it is most likely to buy discs in the 18-24-year-old age group.
It's also not just a question of picking hobbyists, it's emotional: older vinyl buyers are a little more likely to have their emotions (56% vs 53%) and enjoy being alone (69% vs 66%).
But are YouGov's results true?
If this blog were a news report on television, the camera would follow me as I was walking in the middle of a busy street in Soho, wearing a modest grey suit. I’d blink lively before I would stop, form a cup in my hands and say something authoritative, such as “Let’s take a closer look at it.”
Let's take a closer look.
At the same time, when Soho's charm and dirt are slowly being removed - hotels, chain restaurants and shops, as well as office supply stores, are now filling buildings where there used to be independent shops - a handful of stunning specialized record stores are still left.
For the next section, I would probably ask the camera to stop filming for a while as I stand very close to different men in some of these stores when trying to judge by observing their age and their emotional temperament.
Stuart from Glasgow, 55, followed by Sister Raysta (calculator: two young women, three middle-aged men) to Reckless Records (calculator: eight middle-aged men, one woman, probably in their twenties), spend their afternoon in London for a meeting, and goes around the shops to spend a few hours.
I'll ask if he's a collector.
‘I amuse myself,’ he said.
I've got about 3000 or 4000 records.
These plates, which are shown in the picture above (he sent a sample by e-mail, I didn't follow him home), cover the walls of his living room.
The reason for obtaining such a huge number of albums and singles is partly due to his disposable income: now he can buy records that he could not afford when they were originally released, and he can buy again the ones he sold when he was young and moneyless.
“Most of the material I bought is from the late 60’s and the early 70’s, albums that were released when there were 11 or 12 records that I was probably a little too young to acquire,” he says.
He agrees that record buyers are more likely introverters who enjoy their own company, and adds: “I don’t smoke and don’t drink much.
“It’s my vice.”
The 25-year-old Wez, who works for Sister Ray, also believes that many of the customers he encounters fit into YouGov's profile.
However, she has noticed a new wave of people who have been influenced by the enthusiasm of the media. People who have heard that the albums have come into fashion again and for whom there has been a need to buy back their old albums.
Based on the conversations I've had, people have gotten rid of their collections usually in 1998 or 1999.
Clients who sold their vinyl to build CDs are now selling their CDs to buy their records back,” he says.
This may explain an increase in the number of vinyl awakeners of certain ages.
But what about the alleged emotional significance of the records?
It takes a few seconds in a store like Phonica to realize that record stores provide a comforting community for like-minded people.
According to Wez, some older customers have been particularly offended by the contactless payment system.
Some vinyl fans can be overwhelmed by the digital world and choose to rely on the permanence of familiar album covers; objects that evoke the memories of the happy days of their teens.
‘I think sometimes it could be about filling a gap or some kind of comfort in owning items,’ Wez says.
As a collector myself, I admit that this is true, and I believe it is true for many people.
It's an easy way to fill your mind.
‘We’re like their social workers!’ another employee suddenly said from the record stack.
An April poll conducted by ICM revealed that nearly 50% of the people who had bought a vinyl record in the previous month had not yet listened to it.
The survey also found that 41% have a turntable that they never use, while 7% of vinyl buyers don't have a turntable.
At this point of the news report I'd probably look seriously worried when I was scrolling throut the bossa nova collections.
This ‘prize’ aspect of the revival is something that Jonny, 42, who also works in Sounds of the Universe (calculator: three middle-aged men), has noticed.
“Someone came in a while ago and said, ‘I don’t have a record player, but I want to buy Radiohead’s record so we can put it on the shelf,’” he says.
It's not a big part, but it's definitely going to happen.
More product, less music.
We have people who say, ‘I don’t have a record player.’
So not the parents, but the younger people, the late teenagers, who are just getting excited about it.
Foppi, which nowadays has almost an entire floor reserved for vinyls, has a much more diverse audience upon arrival.
Couples in their 70s, tourists taking photos, teenage boys and middle-aged women.
Although the atmosphere is less relaxed than in the stores mentioned earlier - the loudspeakers are the freshest synth and sex-filled pop from Wild Beast, and it's nice to see different customers.
None of them look particularly lonely, but it's hard to prove the gnawing psychological trauma by standing next to someone for 30 seconds.
Elanora, 27, has been walking around Fopp for a few minutes, looking at the various albums for sale.
He's doing window shops, not spending a lot of money.
He says he doesn't make much money, so record collecting isn't really an option.
“It’s expensive.
It’s easy to listen to music either on the computer or through something else, but the beauty of vinyl is...” she sighs in love.
‘I don’t know how to explain — it’s truly unique.’
My last stop is said to have awakened a new generation of vinyl lovers: Urban Outfitters.
While I'm trying to look like I'm researching denim shorts, I'm observing a group of teenage girls who briefly gather around Polaroid cameras before my father and daughter contemplate the purchase of Crosley's record player.
On the left is a staircase with a staircase with the albums of Adele, Jeff Buckley, Amy Winehouse and Fleetwood Mac: a mixture of contemporary music and classics, the essential albums of any average collection.
No one looks at them – perhaps because they’re just ornaments, and some are so high that it would be impossible to touch them.
It’s purely ‘art vinyls’.
But considering that even the slightest attention received by these items may mean more money for the industry and send a few customers to Soho's depth to get more, there's not much to complain about here.
It should come as no surprise that the population that is more likely to have more time and money is also the one who spends the most on luxury items, such as vinyls.
But while my findings suggest that YouGov's results are accurate at least in Soho's record stores, they don't rule it out the large number of female collectors and vinyl lovers, which exists in the real world, record stores and online forums.
These people are likely to work hard in school or office.
They certainly don't spend their Thursday mornings wandering around Berwick Street's record stores, browsing through records and avoiding the eyes of a creepy, 30-year-old fake television presenter as he peeks over the shoulders of unsuspecting middle-aged men.
