In Flow, megalomania is a virtue
The exact criteria and ambitious visions for comfort have established Flow Festival's status as an internationally acclaimed event starring the world's top artists.
Flow Festival starts on Friday in Helsinki.
Festival visitors have typically expressed themselves with their distinctive clothing.
One of the main stage's artists this year is punk legend Iggy Pop.
Flow Festival's event area is styled, for example, with artificial turf and art installations.
It has never been enough for us to just have a main stage and a beer tent, says Tuomas Kallio, the artistic director of the event.
Flow Festival in Suvilahti, Helsinki this weekend, has grown from a small corte festival to an event for tens of thousands of visitors.
There is a simple question behind the success: What kind of event would the creators want to go to?
It's quite unusual in event productions to think like this.
Often, the goal is to achieve the level of necessity and act in accordance with official regulations, says Tuomas Kallio, the artistic director of the event.
He knew a week before the event that Flow was going to sell out again this year.
The aim has been to increase the number of visitors of last year's 70 000 people by 5 000.
This year, the festival will feature a number of interesting Finnish and foreign artists.
An increase in the number of visitors is a good thing, because you have to finance the activities in some way.
On the other hand, we don't want to grow too much, because at some point the mass would be like a herd of cattle, which is controlled in huge fairways.
We don't want that.
Over the course of 13 years, the ideology of a successful event has been inflated in such a way that there are 130 performers, art installations and numerous restaurant points and drinking bars in the area partially decorated with artificial turf.
In addition to the main stage and smaller stages, the venue this year is a tent of 15 000 people.
Kallio admits that the event could be carried out more simply.
Maybe I'm a bit megalomaniac, but we've been able to preserve Flow's life.
If it's lost, it won't be our thing anymore.
For Kallio, the greatest joy and reward for the work done is that even big visions are practically possible today.
In the early years of the event in the middle of the 2000's, the operations were very dependent on the City of Helsinki's support of approximately 20 000 euros.
Last year, the limited liability company running Flow had a turnover of approximately six million euros.
It can be used to create "wretched things", such as the construction of that 73-meter-wide tent.
Flow's visitors have typically expressed themselves with a distinctive outfit.
In addition to the music event, we can talk about a community of trendy young adults.
However, the event has not consciously been profiled as particularly fashionable.
Above all, we want to stand out with content.
However, fashion phenomena also match Flow's values: curiosity and desire to interact with the environment, says Kallio.
This year, one of the main stage's artists is punk legend, 69-year-old Iggy Pop
The man has a huge group of fans in his own genre, but he is not the most typical performer at a festival that combines indie rock, pop music, rap and soul.
According to Kallio, Iggy was booked, because this is probably the last time we'll see him on a gig in Finland.
We're all interested, history and the future.
It's not just what's on the commercial radio.
Iggy's latest album is damn good and she's done some damn good gigs in the last few years.
