Pamela Tola receives huge compensation from the mould house
Actor Pamela Tola and her former husband, CEO Pauli Waroma, have won a fierce dispute over the apartment they bought in Espoo in 2009.
The purchase price of the two-storey detached house had been 330 000 euros.
However, after making the transaction, a serious moisture problem was revealed in the house, which caused microbial growth that is dangerous to health.
The couple had to sell the apartment in good condition in 2014.
The sales price had fallen to 132 000 euros, so Tola and Waroma accrued as much as 198 000 euros in financial losses.
The couple took the matter to court and demanded a price reduction from the seller.
The seller denied the claim, but Tola and Waroma won the case in both the District Court and the Court of Appeal.
The Court of Appeal ruled on Wednesday.
It upheld the District Court's judgment ordering the seller to pay Tola and Waroma a price reduction of 150 000 euros.
In addition, the seller will have to pay a total of 47 344 euros for the couple's legal and settlement costs.
The court moderated the price reduction slightly, because some of the need to repair the apartment had already been known to buyers when making the housing transaction.
However, according to the Court of Appeal, the building defects that occurred were materially more serious than what had been presented.
Tola was looking for a home in good condition
Tola told the Court of Appeal that the couple had been looking for a home in good condition because they didn't want to do repair work.
He had the impression that the building had been renovated and in good condition, and that the factors mentioned in the condition inspection report were not harmful.
The condition inspection report mentioned the risks and shortcomings of ventilation and the fact that the management of precipitation and melting waters flowing along the rock surface is not sufficient.
However, on the basis of the discussions, it did not turn out that it was a matter of urgency.
Tola and Waroma were left with the impression that the rainwater coming from the cliff should be controlled a bit within a few years.
The transaction was made in October 2009.
Within a few years it emerged that the moisture problems in the apartment were much more serious than expected.
Inspections carried out in 2012 and 2013 included microbial vegetation, which, according to witnesses, were caused both by rock rainwater and capillary-rising soil moisture.
On the western side of the house, microbial-contaminated air could, according to the inspector's assessment, also get indoors through the porch.
Mold or yeast that enables the health risk and exceeds the limit values were found in brush samples in the utility room, basement staircase, living room window bay and basement washroom.
The terraces were built without permits
The court also disputed whether the apartment had been expanded in violation of the building permit.
The court ruled that Tola and Waroma were not aware of the City of Espoo's decision, according to which changes had been made to the apartment in violation of the permit.
Among other things, the terraces had been built, even though there were no proper permits yet.
However, mold problems were a central issue in court.
According to the Court of Appeal, on the basis of the information received from the seller prior to the transaction, the buyers could assume that the building was suitable for living as such and that there was no need for extensive repair work in the near future.
According to Tola and Waroma, the Court of Appeal had no particular reason to carry out a more extensive inspection than usual.
Buyers have not failed to comply with their reporting obligations, the Court of Appeal states in its judgment.
Terrible archive discovery: Hundreds of thousands of women wanted to give birth to Nazis - also Finns in the register
Journalist Christopher Andersson writes in a recent Dagens Nyheter article how documents have been recorded on microfilms in Maryland in the United States for a total of 218 500 women, all of whom wanted to marry SS men.
The majority of the women are German, but there are also Americans, the French, the Dutch and the Russians.
In addition, at least 65 Danes, 86 Norwegians and 14 Finns and 31 Swedish women wanted to be the Nazi Brides.
The documents and license plates come from the Racial Agency, which operated in Nazi Germany.
The Racial Bureau served under SS Director Heinrich Himmler.
No SS man in Nazi Germany was allowed to have children until he was examined by an SS doctor.
The same was true for women who were supposed to be able to give birth to more than 10 children.
According to Andersson, gynecological examinations were carried out for women to investigate the matter.
Among other things, the Racial Bureau investigated the women's backgrounds by examining church books.
The purpose was to make sure that there were no Jews in the women's family tree.
Women also had to be prepared to give up their Swedish citizenship and to give the breed agency handwritten biographies and good recommendations from their loved ones.
The Racial Bureau wanted assurances that the women were "child-loved", "savaging", and "good National Socialists" who knew German well.
Women who did not meet the requirements were not accepted as Brides of SS men.
The article does not say what happened to the 14 Finnish women found in the archive's documents.
SS executives created a state-subsidized program known as Lebensborn, in 1936.
Among other things, the program stipulated that each SS man should have four children, either in or outside of marriage.
Lebensborn homes take care of children born out of wedlock and their mothers.
However, the programme was not actively promoted, according to the US Holocaust Museum's website.
On the other hand, Nazi population policy focused on family and marriage.
The state encouraged marriage by offering loans and support for having children.
The German mother's honorary cross was awarded to women who had had at least four children.
In Nazi Germany, marriages between "Aryans" and "non-Aryans" were prohibited by law, the museum's website states.
