Jari Sillanpää's Satulinna was recorded for a couple of hours of sleep: "I was so hung up"
Jari Sillanpää has been one of Finland's favorite artists for more than 20 years.
Sillanpää, who has become the best-selling solo artist in Finland and the star of dance halls and festivals, is still on the brink, and he is currently on the rise.
Mari Koppinen's book Jari Sillanpää - Paljaana was published today on Thursday.
In the book Sillanpää talks about his life and career, both good and bad.
Sillanpää's career rose to a total ascension when he defeated Tango King's crown at Seinäjoki Tango Market in 1995.
Sillanpää sang tango in the competition, but he had already planned a little different music for himself at the time.
In the spring of 1995, Jukka Kuoppamäki had composed a tango piece for Sillanpää at the request of the man, which Kassu Halonen arranged in a slightly different format.
The song Satulinna became one of the most popular songs in Finnish musical history.
However, according to the book, the recording of the song didn't go quite as planned: Sillanpää sang it on tape with a terrible hangover and after only a couple of hours of sleep.
We'd had a party tonight.
I was in such a hangover!
Sillanpää recalls in the book.
You wouldn't immediately guess from the recording of the song, which has become a huge hit, that it has been sung with a hangover.
Even though I wasn't physically at my best, it was still good.
I learned that you don't have to insist on being perfect and perfect all the time.
Another incident related to the man's most most popular songs, which is described in the book, took place almost twenty years later.
In 2013 Sillanpää divorced his then-partner and the case eventually ended up in the texts of the song You Deserve Gold, which was released as a single in 2014.
According to the book, the song words ordered from Saara Törmänen were not what Sillanpää would have wanted.
Sillanpää had imagined that he was making an album that tells a true story about his own life, but the lyrics of a song about a break-up weren't true. Instead, it was written based on the news from 7 Days magazine.
According to the newspaper's news, Jari's and his ex-believed breakup was told that Jari's excessive partying and alcohol consumption was told, but according to Jari, it was complete nonsense and was not true.
According to the book, Jari called the record company and refused to sing the song, but ended up singing it - and that's a good thing.
In June, the Britons surprised themselves by voting their empire out of the EU.
In the end, 52 % of voters voted in favour and 48% would have remained in the Union, even though polls of the week predict the opposite outcome.
Britain's economists and economic institutions were almost unanimous in favour of remaining in the EU.
No prestigious research institute or university flagged for the difference, even though a couple of individual researchers dared to disagree with the mainstream of their profession.
The basic mantra of Western politics, the thematic slogan "It's the economy, stupid" by former U.S. President of former U.S
Economists were shocked when their voices were not heard.
They were shocked when the economy didn't seem to be the most important issue in politics.
Economical curly language
In its most recent monthly survey, the Centre for Macroeconomics provides responses to the Brexit disaster.
The most typical answer is that economists were not listened to, because this time the voters made the decision on grounds other than economic.
One of economists' explanation for the loss of the position of the economy is that people don't understand the economy.
Many still think that the number of jobs is independent of the number of working people, almost constant.
If an immigrant gets a job, it's from the real Briton.
Many also have the impression that a decrease in the value of the currency would be a good thing, because it promotes the export industry.
According to the Economist Council, the voters did not believe the economic arguments, because the consensus of economists did not come up enough.
In particular, they were disgusted by the fairness of the BBC, which provided equal space for only a couple of per cent of economist economists to support the withdrawal from the EU as to the overwhelming majority.
The EU-hostile press was not interested in explaining what IFS, IFM, BoE, CEPR and LSE and other economic letter combinations about the consequences of the resignation are.
Those distant combinations of letters spoke facelessly about economic growth, trade relations, sovereign debt and currency.
And if economist's messages were both listened to and understood, they were not cared about, because economists represent an elite with whom the voters do not have common interests.
Economist-like academics in big cities would have stayed in the EU, but in small towns and the countryside the message did not work.
Immigration is the economy, too
The overwhelming victory argument of the Brexit vote concerned immigration.
An immigrant will take your job, an immigrant will congest your hospital, an immigrant will take advantage of you.
These arguments worked.
Each café has foreign workers.
How many jobs would the British have if they left?
You can see immigrants in the waiting room of every health centre.
How much easier would it be for the Briton to get doctor's appointments if they didn't exist?
There are children of immigrants in every school.
How much easier it would be for real British children to get a place to school if they could get rid of them?
There are foreigners living in every county.
How much more space would the natives have if they left?
I think these are economic issues.
They apply to jobs, housing and public services.
Economists, however, did not succeed in responding to them, and if they did, their responses did not gain visibility.
Economists were unable to explain that, thanks to immigrants, Britons have even more jobs, fewer queues of health centres and more money for education and social security.
Immigrants - and especially EU immigrants - are net contributors to Britain's general government finances and their consumption creates jobs.
Aberdeen's Real Estate Fund prepares for Brexit in cash
One of the funds was Aberdeen Asset Management, whose real estate investments are managed by Pertti Vanhanen from Finland.
According to him, Aberdeen prepared in time for Brexit by drawing up plans A and B.
A few weeks before the vote, a few weeks before the voting for Plan ‘B’, i.e. a camp in favour of leaving, began to be prepared.
The concern was that investors would start withdrawing their funds from the market.
Real estate funds normally do not have large liquid assets, but now they might be necessary.
We started to increase the cash.
When Brexit took place on the 23rd day, we had 25 per cent of the fund's investments in cash or liquid assets, Vanhanen says about Aberdeen's one of Aberdeen's on-day-to-date liable real estate fund, which the news involved.
According to Vanhanen, the normal level would be 15-20 per cent.
At that time, the value of the fund was 3.5 billion pounds.
In total, the company's funds are worth about 20 billion pounds.
It was the highest in nine vacant real estate funds," Vanhanen says of all open real estate funds in the UK.
He estimates that the lowest amount of cash in these funds was ten per cent or even below it.
In other words, real estate funds prepared for Brexit with very different strategies.
The funds closed trading
As the camp, which supports the withdrawal from the EU, Europe went haywire.
However, less than two weeks following the voting in the real estate market were quite peaceful, Vanhanen says.
Two things happened that changed the situation.
The first was a speech by Mark Carney, the governor of the Bank of England, in which he estimated that Brexit could be a big risk for commercial real estate operators.
In addition, the first of real estate funds, Standard Life, froze its trade.
These, together, splashed the situation and put it in the main headlines of the Financial Times and the BBC.
The situation was immediately compared in 2007 and 2008, Vanhanen says.
This made investors nervous.
Institutional investors remained calmer, but private investors were nervous.
The snowball began to spin, and one fund after another had to close its doors.
Aberdeen's situation was good due to the accumulated cash, but it also had to freeze its trade for six days.
According to Vanhanen, they did not actually shut down trading, but changed the pricing downwards.
In practice, investors were given the time to consider whether it is now worthwhile to withdraw the money in a panic.
Aberdeen's message was that if you don't have to take your money out now, it's not worth it, because it's going to make losses.
It reassured the situation, and the majority withdrew the notification.
Since then it has been quite smooth, and now the market has been very peaceful for a couple of weeks, Vanhanen says.
He points out that, for example, there has been no similar turmoil in Aberdeen's continental Europe funds.
However, there were a lot of questionnaires.
Unreasonable attention to real estate funds
According to Vanhanen, the closures of real estate funds' transactions received unreasonably much attention.
Open funds, which were affected by closures, account for about four per cent of Britain's real estate.
Aberdeen's lockdown lasted six days, and the company sold less than ten properties out of 80 properties in London.
Some other funds are still closed.
Aviva Investors warned investors this week that it might keep its funds closed for 2017.
At the moment, there is uncertainty in the air and economic growth projections have been revised downwards, but the situation does not look "ferocious" for Aberdeen.
The biggest issues now concern the future development of the British economy.
Despite the uncertainty, the situation has even brought new opportunities for some real estate investors, Vanhanen points out.
The price of the pound and the dollar is at its weakest in 30 years, in addition to which there is a 15% sale on the market compared to the period prior to the Brexit vote.
Dollar buyers can now buy the same property up to 30 percent cheaper.
There are a lot of willing buyers, Vanhanen says.
Brexit brought minuses to London's real estate market
At two in the morning the situation didn't look like much yet.
But at four o'clock Pertti Vanhanen, who is responsible for the real estate investments of the British real estate fund Aberdeen Asset Management, woke up again to look at the results of the Brexit vote, the situation was different.
That's where the power word came in.
I realized that this is now going to plan B, Vanhanen says in his second home in Helsinki.
He immediately headed to work.
The City of London was already completely alive in the morning.
At the office, TV screens, which have normally been decreed to be silent, shook the news, and the usually loud people were completely silenced when Prime Minister David Cameron stepped in front of the camera in the morning to announce his resignation.
As the market opened, the display terminals flashed nothing but red.
It was the kind of morning that you never thought you'd live," Vanhanen says.
Not because, despite the turmoil, Aberdeen, one of Britain's largest real estate funds, completed two real estate transactions the day after the Brexit vote.
The atmosphere was confused.
However, the company was well prepared for the alternative, which is why the situation was kind of calm, Vanhanen says.
Bresting Voting Begins Correction
Voting was followed by a storm.
The pound collapsed, Britain's leadership went on an exchange.
Drastic declines were anticipated in the market for the real estate markets in the United Kingdom, in particular London.
London's office spaces were anticipated to receive up to 20 per cent minuses in the coming years.
Soon, a number of British real estate funds began to freeze their trade one after the other.
Aberdeen did that for six days.
Now the storm has subsided, but there is still uncertainty in the air.
However, it is not visible on Vanhanen's face, which has spent its last summer holidays this week in Helsinki.
He still thinks it's far too early to say a lot about what's going to happen with Brexit.
So much is still open: how the difference will take place and how Scotland will react to the situation, for example.
The development of the real estate market in London has already been first known.
CBRE's information on price development covers approximately one-third of the market.
According to them, the prices of all properties in London fell by 3.3 per cent from June to July.
London City's office buildings accounted for 6.1 per cent, Vanhanen says.
The numbers aren't that terrible, but they're monthly changes.
The monthly index began to be published in 1987, and there have been inferior figures for only five months, Vanhanen says.
All equal drops coincided with the 2007 and 2008 years of crisis.
It is also interesting because Brexit was compared quite quickly to Lehman's downfall and the global economic crisis that began on the US real estate market.
According to Vanhanen, the comparison is not quite accurate, as it is now a home-made crisis in Britain.
The atmosphere is different, too.
The real estate market has not seen panic sales like that, but the willingness to sell to improve liquidity," Vanhanen says.
Financing is also normally available.
There could have been a corrective maneuver anyway
In fact, the price correction movement is no surprise to him - that could have been ahead even without Brexit.
According to Aberdeen's assessment, the prices of properties in London and the world's number one cities have been too high for years.
We've been saying for two years now that City and especially offices are overpriced," Vanhanen says.
So the fall started really high.
London's prices have climbed upwards for four reasons, Vanhanen lists.
Britain's economy has grown strongly, London's population as well.
Another big trend is that the properties are the last category of ownership that is globalizing, Vanhanen says.
The Middle East and Asia have introduced new players to the market, and an increasing number of institutions and private investors are lookining for real estate investments abroad.
As a well-known city, London is an easy destination.
In addition, prices have been fuelled by low interest rates, due to which, for example, pension companies have had to look for returns on new destinations.
On top of this, the loan has been readily available - although, according to Vanhanen, the leverage has now been used clearly less than during the boom before the 2008 crisis.
Diversify, decentralize
In London, the rise in prices stabilised last summer, Vanhanen says.
Aberdeen also began to lighten its holdings in London, and last year it was a net seller.
There was some kind of turnaround expected, and now it's here.
What from now on?
It depends on how Britain's national economy develops, Vanhanen says.
He doesn't have the courage to judge the future, but he says there's hardly any ascent.
The atmosphere suggests that there is a downward or steady action ahead.
Vanhanen has already seen several movements up and down, as he has been in the real estate industry since 1988.
And he's still an optimist who believes we'll get through the bumps with work.
If you're well-prepared for risks, you're in a better position.
He believes in global decentralization.
If you've invested in one market, you're too much a victim.
If you have diversified, the risks have also been diversified, Vanhanen says.
S Group will start registering purchase information in more detail than before
Soon, S Group will know what you're eating, as the trading group announced today that it will start to collect more detailed information about the purchases of its regular customers.
So far, when information has been collected from the total amount of the receipt, the group will also start picking it up at the beginning of May.
S Group will come after Kesko here, as the information on the goods purchased with a positive card has been collected in Kesko's customer register for a couple of years.
According to Ismo Riitala, CEO of K-Plus Oy, the feedback from the programme has been mainly positive.
Product-specific data is collected with the customer's consent and is used for marketing subject to permission.
The most effective channel is email.
Quite a few customers have prohibited the use of product information, Riitala says to Taloussanomat.
According to him, Kesko does not intend to react to S Group's registration change.
According to grocery shopping alone, Kesko classifies its regular customers into ten groups.
The classification is updated monthly.
Loyal customers are also classified according to the number of purchases and the amount of money spent on them.
The information is used for marketing targeted to the customer.
Product-specific purchase information is only collected from K Group's own chains.
Partners, such as Neste Oil, do not disclose their data.
Lidl Finland does not have a loyalty program and this is not currently planned.
The Finnish Local Deal has its own First Bonus loyalty program, which you can join at Valintatalo, Siwa and Euromarkets.
At the moment, we do not collect product or product-grade information, we will stick to the level of the total amount of the receipt.
In other words, we know in which store the purchases were made and on what date, as well as the customer's receipt
We haven't gone to the same level as K Group or S Group, says Commercial Director Anu Ora.
According to him, the loyalty programme will be renewed in the future, but Lähikauppa will tell about it later.
However, Ora does not promise that product information will be registered.
Initially An Experiment
SOK's co-owner and customer register has been relatively extensive so far.
In addition to personal and address information, the basic data have also included the profession, education, form of housing, the children's years of birth and previous personal data.
Individuals belonging to the same economy and their role have also been registered.
The S-Etukortti card purchase includes the date and time of purchase, the place, the total amount of the purchases and the payment method.
In addition, the information of the S-bonus partners has been obtained from their cash register systems.
From the beginning of May, product-level purchasing information will be stored in the Sokos and Emotion chains.
On the other hand, S-markets, Prismas, Kodin Terros and ABC chains record the data for the time being at the product group level.
For a long time now, customers have hoped that marketing could be targeted more precisely, says Marjaana Saarikoski, SOK's Co-Ower and Partner Operations Manager.
S Group says that it will inform its customers about the change.
They may prohibit the use of product-specific information for marketing.
However, the information is saved at the checkouts anyway, whether you want it or not.
If you are interested, you can check your stored data on S Group's website.
For example, checkouts cannot access the data, emphasises Saarikoski.
Video: Heila-puppy is Customs' newest dog reinforcement - puppies will become the first explosives dog
The black Labrador retriever's puppy vigorously sniffs out five similar boxes placed on the ground in a row at Helsinki Airport.
It won't be long before it sits firmly next to the middle drawer.
Customs inspector Lassi Suntio rewards Heila's dog playing with his favorite toy.
A jar of gunpowder-scented casings were uncovered next to which Heila sat next to.
Gunpowder gas is the first smell that has now been practiced with Heila, says Suntio, the owner of Heila's dog.
Security situation and e-commerce main reasons
Heila is Customs' first dog specialising in the search for weapons and explosives, and it will be placed to work at Helsinki Airport next year.
Tasks may also take Heila and Suntio to other border crossing points and harbours if necessary.
However, the airport will be Heila's main workplace, as the international security situation has changed due to serious terrorist attacks in Europe.
Attacks in Paris and Brussels caused measures at Customs, and surveillance has been strengthened after the strikes, says Mikko Grönberg, Senior Officer at Customs.
Another main reason for the training of weapons and explosives dogs for Customs' duties is postal consignments passing through Helsinki Airport.
With the increased internet trade, up to 40 million postal consignments pass through the airport every year, which may include armament supplies and explosives ordered from abroad.
In recent years, Finnish Customs has made an online deal at Helsinki-VantØtal Airport among purchases, luggage and air cargo of passenger planes with approximately 300 discoveries related to weapons, firearm parts, ammunition and explosives, as well as their precursors.
Heila will work in different parts of Helsinki Airport.
It's going to look at checked baggage and mail items.
It will be of great use in ports in the future, as it can go through large container areas and mark areas that require more detailed research, says Grönberg.
Based on Heila's performance, Customs will assess whether more weapons and explosive dogs will be trained in the future.
Grönberg is hopeful of the idea.
School in Kirkkonummi
Heila-puppy's training is coordinated by the Customs dog school in Kirkkonummi.
Suntio and Heila are also training with police and Finnish Defence Forces experts.
The police have strong experience in training dogs and the Finnish Defence Forces have expertise in explosive substances.
Customs, together with the other authorities, decides what odors Heila is taught to recognize.
Heila is always further trained as needed, says Grönberg.
Heila's colleagues at Helsinki Airport are Customs' six narcotic dogs, as well as Massi, a money and drug dog.
Labrador retriever Heila has been chosen from the litter of ten puppies.
The selection criteria for the dog to be selected for the airport include briskness and hunting instinct, as well as tranquility, because the dog is involved with weapons, explosives and streams of people.
Heila, who lives at Suntio's home, is a close co-worker to a customs inspector, but also a family member.
You can't get anywhere without Heila at home, Suntio laughs.
Subtle orders go to Heila's delivery once and for all.
Ilta-Sanomat's cameraman loses the camera's microphone cover for a fast dog, but Heila restores the cover that looks like a soft toy right from the "let off" command.
Harsh competition for working dogs
Heila's initial training will graduate next year.
There are still level tests ahead, next in October, where it will be assessed whether the training is sufficient for demanding tasks.
Heila can be seen at Helsinki Airport next year if everything goes according to plan.
Unfortunately, passengers cannot come and iron the dog that is wearing a working vest, Suntio reminds.
The cost of one working dog is about 60000-70000 euros per year for Customs.
Their working age is about seven to eight years, up to ten, after which they retire in the care of their owner.
The working days can be long, from eight to ten hours, but the active working parts only last from ten minutes to a couple of hours.
There is fierce competition to own working dogs at Customs.
Heila's owner was about 20-30 Customs employees.
Happiness hit Suntio, who has never had a working dog before, but he has years of experience in dogs and control duties at Customs.
Mini-state receives its first Olympic medal - the people get a day off
The Fiji Olympic gold medal in rugby, with a population of less than a million, is valued so high in the country that the government has sentenced August 22 as a national holiday.
According to the country's broadcaster, Fiji's government confirmed this after the championship was decided.
The day off will be taken the day after the team's homecoming.
There will also be celebrations for the hero team in their home country.
Fiji's rugby team celebrated the championship.
According to The Guardian, the country watched the finals in the capital Suva, where a screen had been erected in the stadium.
Normally, only native Fijians are interested in rugby, but this game united our multicultural community.
Even in Chinese restaurants watched the game, I heard them cheer," Maikele Seru, a reporter for the Fiji Times, told the Guardian.
