The people of Japan prefer the Nordic countries to Finland - tourists are attracted by the Northern Lights: It is possible to see a fairy-tale mirror aurora in the autumn
The purpose of the campaign is to attract Japanese people to start Northern Lights trips already in the early autumn, Visit Finland says in a press release.
The main destinations of Japanese Northern Lights tourism are Inari and Levi.
The Northern Lights are the trump cards of our winter tourism, and they are strongly associated with the heart winter and snowy landscapes.
There is a lot of demand for Northern Lights trips in Lapland, but we are now trying to increase the sales of autumn trips.
Few people know that the Northern Lights season starts from early autumn and continues well into the spring.
In the autumn, it is possible to see the so-called mirror aurora, where the surface of the lake reflects the Northern Lights, making the sight two times fairy-tale-like, says Anne Lind, Acting Marketing Manager at Visity Finland.
Visit Finland's Northern Lights campaign's main target group consists of the wealthy 25-35-year-old Japanese women living in the city, dreaming of tourism and new experiences.
They are already interested in Finland, its nature and design.
Tourists are lured to Finland, for example, through online advertising.
According to a survey carried out by researchers at Aalto University and the universities of Turku and the universities of Tokyo, Finland has an exceptionally strong and positive country image among the Japanese compared to the other Nordic countries.
However, the Japanese would need clearer unique sights from Finland, and here the Northern Lights have huge potential, according to Lind.
Overnight stays by Japanese tourists in Finland have clearly increased more than in the other Nordic countries.
Last year, they spent more than twice as many overnight stays in Finland as in Sweden, Denmark or Norway.
From the point of view of Finnish tourism, the Japanese are an interesting target group, as they spent more than 80 million euros in Finland.
On average, the Japanese spent 183 euros a day in Finland, Lind says.
Britain settles for rugby silver as Fid's masterclass leads gold
Great Britain's impressive advance in the rugby tournament plummeted into Fidzi's final challenge in Thursday night's final, when the team suffered a 43-7 crushing defeat at Deodoro's stadium here in Rio.
Fidzi organized a masterclass in the handling, chewing, dodging, support game, running lines, and sheer power when he won gold - their first Olympic medal.
The defeat came with a reasonable consolation about the silver medal - the third of Britain's Day - and they got to be proud of their hard work in the Olympic rugby's kick-off tournament, where they defeated New Zealand, Argentina and South Africa.
Earlier on the same day's semi-final's tough 7-5 victory over South Africa may have bothered them in the final.
But in spite of this, Fidi would have defeated even a top team of 15 people in this condition.
It may - but probably no - comfort the fact that at least one Briton has a gold medal around his neck tomorrow: Fidi's head coach is Ben Ryan, England's former boss Wimbledon.
It's been quite a trip to the UK.
Eight years ago, coached by Ryan, they lost every game in the Adelaide tournament.
Here, they only lost one.
This spanking hurts, but it's startling.
Fidi started excellently, rejectinginging Britain's tackles for fun.
For Captain Tom Mitchell and his team, this was as if they had been running on moving buses, and the world champions led 5-0 after Osea Kolinisau dived into the end zone in the corner.
After four minutes on the clock, Jerry Tuwai's second touchdown on the right wing resulted in a goal kick.
12-0, and Great Britain had barely touched the ball.
Mitchell's superb tackle stopped the man-mountain Leone Nakarawa's third goal, but it was just a sluggish for Fidi.
After seven minutes, Jasa Veremalua scored on the right-handed corner, and Great Britain had a 17-0 loss.
Nakarawa scored again from a new start, and there was still more than a minute left on the watch...the first half time.
Vatemo Ravouvou then plunged through the UK from loose tackles and rushed to the line to score.
After the goalie, the situation was 29-0.
It looked as if Fidi had been born to play rugby, while Great Britain was just learning the game.
And that's actually the case.
During the break, Great Britain made a lot of changes that blocked Fidi's dam for a full four minutes, which was good compared to the first half until Josua Tuisova scored Fidi's impressive pass game.
Dan Norton snatched a goal to the Brind in the left corner of the touchdown and narrowed the situation to 36-7, but the game had long been lostted to the islanders of the South Sea, whose supporters sang, swung flags and danced in the stands.
It was tricky not to admire Fidi's joyful approach.
Mata Viliame with her last-minute goal was frosting on Fidi's cake, which didn't need frosting.
Whistled, the Fidians hugged and Great Britain fell on the lawn, completely defeated.
Earlier on the same day, Fidi had beaten Japan in the semi-final 20-5, and Great Britain defeated South Africa in a tight semi-final, where each scored one goal - Britain's goal-kick decided the game.
Kyle Brown took South Africa's lead in the first half, but Dan Norton crossed the scoring zone with dazzling legwork and scored a goal in the second half.
A mighty late tackle from Marcus Watson, the brother of England's 15-aside rugby team Anthony, secured the victory and finally a silver medal.
Helen Glover and Heather Stanning were defeated by Helen Glover and Heather Stanning
There won't be more emotional gold in these races, and that really means something.
From the first bet to the last one, 220 hard-fought-times towards the history books, Helen Glover and Heather Stanning found a deeper meaning in their golden moment at Estacio da Lagoa.
Even though they rowed alone, there were three votes on the boat. The echo of coach Robin Williams was present all the time, when the duo competed for their second consecutive Olympic gold medal in a women's pair competition - the first women in the history books of British sport to achieve such an accomplishment.
Their control was perfect, and this was their 39th appearance invincible, which is also record-breaking.
This, what we are seeing, two glorious athletes gliding on the surface with the grace of the swan, not entirely reflecting the nature of victory.
Behind each rowing gold is a group of helpful pairs of hands that gradually contribute to the things that make a difference. And no pair of hands was as essential as that of a coach, who, 30 months ago after being diagnosed with cancer, didn’t know if he would live to this day.
As he said, it was a close-knit situation.
I had bladder cancer, which is one of the things that could be cut, and they performed surgery.
If they don't, it'll spread everywhere.
It could've been pretty bad.
It'll change your way of thinking a bit.
Williams was diagnosed in December 2013.
The trio were lucky enough to be able to start his treatment at the beginning of 2014, when they caused as little disruption as possible - if that expression could ever be used in situations like this.
I had to get paid, so I had to heal quickly.
It encouraged me.
I was in the hospital and I was walking around the ward - 20, 30, 50 feet at a time when they said that a few steps would be enough.
That's how it started.
Rowing itself is a trip, but that's what makes it a little bigger.
Immediately after the victory, the first of the couple's thoughts was Williams, who had had a nervous morning, and he had spoken a little more than usual, trying to deal with his own nervousness.
Stanning, the Afghan command-experiencing captain of the royal artillery regiment, said: “I’ve been an emotional wreck this week, which may be a little overreaction, but this means so much to me.”
It only emphasizes how important this is to me, how hard I and Helen, and Robin have worked.
The past three years with Helen and Rob have been great.
If it wasn't for Robin, we wouldn't be anything, so he deserves a big thank you, he's the best coach in the world.
I couldn't have imagined a better training partner or a better coach.
Helen Glover and Heather Stanning haven't lost the race for five years.
The pair set a tough rhythm that their rivals couldn't reach.
Even in the last 500 meters, when New Zealand's and Danish teams began to approach, Stanning and Glover had too much energy in storage.
We were very closed in an instant.
Helen shouted ‘stay in the process, stick to the process.’
It's really important to keep control.
We had a good start and didn't try to do anything special at the same time.
We weren't looking for fireworks today, we're just trying to win a good rowing and winning the race, and that's what we did.
A couple is looking forward to a year before deciding on their future.
Glover will be attending the wedding next month when he marries TV adventurer Steve Backshall.
Right now he just wants to enjoy the moment.
“I didn’t want to say that this would be my last race.
It would've put too much pressure.
It took about six months to realize that I was an Olympic champion.
Everything was so new and I was so stressed.
It lasted about four minutes here.
It feels so much better,” he said.
Iceland replaces the government stormed by Panama leaks
Iceland will hold early parliamentary elections in October, demanded by protesters infuriated by the Panama leaks already in the spring.
The so-called Panama Papers revealed that several Icelandic politicians evaded taxes with the help of tax havens.
Prime Minister Sigmundur David Gunnlaugsson resigned because of the scandal, but it was not enough for the protesters.
His successor, Sigurdur Ingi Johannsson, then promised to hold new parliamentary elections in the autumn.
