The quality of bathing waters in the Oulu region passed through a sieve - in Pyykösjärvi, blue-green algae flash
Water samples taken from the official beaches of the Oulu region meet the quality requirements set for bathing water.
Only blue-green algae have been observed at Pyykkösjärvi beach.
During Tuesday and Wednesday, the Oulu Region's environmental services took water samples from nine EU beaches in the Oulu region and 30 from other public beaches.
With the exception of Meriniemi beach, the concentrations of E.coli and intestinal enterococcal bacteria in the bathing waters of all bathing areas fell below the operational limits laid down in the decrees of the Ministry of Social Affairs and Health.
E.coli and enterococcal concentrations in Meriniemi beach water were ensured on Wednesday, when the bacterial concentrations had fallen belowen the action limits.
The blue-green algae sludge observed at the Pyykösjärvi beach does not yet make the water unsuitable, but in favorable conditions the situation can quickly turn into blue-green algae flowering, in which case blue-green algae mass is formed on the water surface as ferries.
The environmental measure monitors the situation with more frequent inspections.
If blue-green algae blossom turns into blue-green algae flowering, this will also be announced separately on the beach bulletin board.
See the results of the bathing water samples here.
MTK's Hakkarainen on forest gift deduction: More than 80 per cent of forest owners outside
The forest gift deduction is one tax change in the Ministry of Finance's budget proposal for next year.
A tax deduction is proposed for the Income Tax Act, which would be granted on the basis of gift tax on a generational change of a forest estate.
In the Ministry of Finance's proposal, the forest gift reduction has four objectives: to promote the generational changes of forest holdings, to rejuvenate the age structure of forest owners, to increase the size of forest holdings and to increase the supply of wood.
The Ministry's aim is to promote entrepreneurial forestry.
A forest owner would make a deduction from pure capital income from forestry.
In general, the idea of creating such a tool to promote the generational changes of forest holdings is good, comments Juha Hakkarainen, Forest Director of the Confederation of Finnish Agricultural Producers and Forest Owners (MTK).
However, Hakkarainen finds something to criticise in the tax change proposal.
It is proposed to make a forest gift deduction from the forest owner's clean capital income.
It's capital income, from which all other deductions have been deducted.
According to MTK and Hakkarainen, the effectiveness of the tax deduction would be higher if the deduction could be made directly from the proceeds from the sale of wood.
The Ministry's proposal starts with making a deduction from the pure capital income of forestry prevents the loss of capital income in forestry, which would be deducted in other taxation.
Such a person would get the tax benefit, even if he did not sell the wood.
The calculation model is too complicated.
The average forest owner is unable to perceive it, says Hakkarainen.
In addition, according to him, the model only applies to a small number of forest owners.
The tax deduction limit tied to forest's yield value is 40 000 euros in the proposal.
According to Hakkarainen, it has been pulled too high.
According to his opinion, it eliminates 82 per cent of Finnish forest owners from the deduction.
The model would have an impact on about 10 per cent of forest owners, he says.
The Ministry of Finance considers that the limit guarantees the goal of increasing the farm size.
According to Hakkarainen, forest gift deductions have been carried out in forest policy for decades.
It is due to the ageing of forest owners.
According to MTK, the average age of forest owners is currently more than 60 years, and about half of forest owners are pensioners.
A powerful hailstorm surprised motorists in Himanka - two cars plunged into a ditch and collided with each other
There was a peculiar rear-end collision in Himanga, Kalajoki, on Friday morning.
According to the Rescue Department, the two cars that drove in a row plunged into a ditch, after which the person behind him collided in front of him.
According to on-call fire chief Harri Pyyhtää, the ditch is suspected to have been caused by a strong hailstorm.
There were two people in the car, both of whom were transported to a hospital for treatment.
There's no further information on their injuries.
The driver of the car in the forecast was not injured.
The accident happened on Highway 8 from Himanga a few kilometres towards Kalajoki.
A Finnish study can help improve the effectiveness of cancer treatments
The recent research results of researchers at the University of Turku can help to make cancer treatments even more effective.
Observations made by Academy Professor Johanna Ivaska's research group show that the hardness of a cancer tumour increases the malignancy of cancer.
Many human tissues, such as breast tissue, are very soft, and tissue cells grow as usual in such an environment.
According to Ivaska, the growth of the cancer could be prevented under laboratory conditions by restoring the tissue surrounding the cancer to correspond to normal, soft tissue.
In the future, it may be possible to improve the effectiveness of drug therapies of cancer by concentrating not only cancer cells, but also on the interaction between cells and the surrounding tissue.
The results of the study were published on 4 August in the prestigious journal Nature Communications in the field of cell biology.
