Ice shark is the world's longest-lived vertebrate, only multiplies at the age of 156
An ice shark has been confirmed as the world's longest-living vertebrate, according to a study published in the journal Science.
In the northern parts of the Atlantic, such as Greenland, an ice shark can live up to 400 years.
The female does not multiply until the age of 156.
The lifespan of an ice shark is increased by the fact that it grows only a centimeter a year.
Only one species of clam is known to live longer than ice sharks.
Scientists found out their age with radiocarbon dating.
Due to nuclear tests carried out in the 1950's, there were radioactive residues in some sharks.
Based on them, the researchers were able to estimate the age of sharks.
The age of the eldest was estimated to be 392 years - although throwing one way or the other can be up to 120 years.
