Plunging flow occurs in a thundery cloud when rainwater and its weight trigger a declining air flow.
Changes in the state of water in a thundery cloud sequester heat, and thus the declining air mass cools and its momentum towards the ground accelerates.
According to the Finnish Meteorological Institute, there will be several downstream currents in Finland during the summer.
It's hard to predict the pathway for downpours.
