Air pollution ages the Londoners
According to the study, the skin in London ages faster than usual.
The cause of skin aging is air pollution.
A British study is reported by the Evening Standard.
The British skincare company Caci analyzes London without the most toxic fine particles and their effects on the skin.
The results show that the skins of many pensioners in London are two and a half years older than, for example, a peer living in the countryside.
Skin aginging particles are very fine dust pollutant, which comes from transport fuels.
According to the Evening Standard, the same particles also cause respiratory diseases, in which, according to the Evening Standard, nearly 9,500 people in London die every year.
Investigations have found that the particles are small enough to penetrate the outer layer of the skin.
Cac's study found that early aging is a risk, especially for Londoners who commute on foot near crowded streets.
Residents of London can try to protect themselves from air pollution, for example, with a variety of masks, but regular skin cleansing is recommended as a more practical solution.
