For the first time, Bulgaria will report inflation in eight months
National statistics show that inflation in Bulgaria was 1% in July.
This is the highest figure in years (since June 2012) and the first reading above zero after October of last year, when a positive 0.2% was reported.
The annual average inflation, measured by the cost-of-living index, was -0.8% in the last 12 months (Augus 2015-July 2016) when compared to the previous 12 months (August 2014 to July 2015).
The cost-of-living index of July 2016 was 101.0% compared to June 2016, i.e. monthly inflation was 1.0%.
From the beginning of the year (July 2016 compared to December 2015), the inflation rate has been -0.2%, and the annual inflation rate in July 2016 was -0.2% compared to July 2015.
Compared to the previous month, in July 2016, the price of the following consumer goods was seen to increase: food and non-alcoholic beverages (1.7%), transport (1.8%), leisure and culture (6.3%), restaurants and hotels (1.2%), miscellaneous goods and services (0.3%).
Prices decreased in clothing and footwear (2.2%), furniture, household supplies and routine maintenance (0.5%), health (0.1%).
Prices remained at the same level as the previous month for alcoholic beverages and tobacco, housing, water, electricity, gas and other fuels, communication and education.
Snapchat ‘anime’ effect criticized as Asian caricature
According to Snapchat, the anime has served as a source of inspiration for the filter, but according to some observers, it is racist.
With its lens tool, Snapchat enables approximately 150 million daily users to shape reality and play with identity in ways that are almost ridiculous.
You can turn yourself into a pineapple, a dog, or a figure that would be suitable for Roy Lichtenstein's painting.
Lenses are simple, face-wracking tools that produce more than 30 million enhanced selfies a day.
All missteps quickly end up in public documents.
Snapchat lenses have previously been criticized with accusations that the app promotes a blackface or “black-face” mask or favors bleached skin color as a beauty ideal.
So when Snapchat this week advertised a lens for some users who gave them crooked eyes, crooked teeth and chubby cheeks, some critics called it a racist caricature of Asian people – a ‘yellow face’.
And they wondered if these recurring controversies could be a sign of the company's bigger problem of diversity.
News and disapproval spread widely on Wednesday with reports from The Verge and Motherboard, a day later Snapchat said it had discontinued the feature.
The company offered an explanation: The lens was meant as a tribute to the anime characters, not a caricature of Asians.
But for observers who experienced racism, the lens was a reminder of offensive stereotypes in action.
Others vehemently banned the anime comparison.
Grace Sparapani, a Korean-American art student whose tweet about the photographs was widely shared, said in an e-mail that the lens was “at least offensive and uncomfortable.”
He added that “it’s tricky to argue against a comparison with aggravated Asian caricature and filter effects next to each other.”
It indicates that the filter is not only a yellow face, but a yellow-faced when taken to extreme degradiness.
Snapchat isn't the only company that's going throut over these cultural traps.
American culture seems to be caught up in an endless struggle for diversity and inclusion, from corporate meeting rooms to Hollywood and devices that we all carry in our hands.
And Snapchat's huge, racially larger-than-expansive audience may mean they're even more likely to look forward to an understanding attitude.
25-year-old Katie Zhu said in an interview on Thursday that when one of Snapchat’s lenses creates an insulting image of the user, ‘it’s much more difficult for these guys to be discreent.’
On Thursday, Zhu, a product manager and engineer working for Medium, decided to delete his Snapchat account and urged others to do the same.
In his writing to Medium and a phone interview, he said he believed that race-related disputes reflected the lack of diversity in Snapchat's hiring practices.
Zhu criticized the company's predominant white male management and ended his writing with the hashtag #DeleteSnapchat (#RemoveSnapchat)
“Either they don’t have a diverse representation of different skin colors in their staff, which is why they’re able to make decisions like this,” Chinese-American Zhu said, “or they’ve have representatives of different skin colors at work, but they’ren’t in positions where they’d feel safe or be able to speak directly.”
Other observers share his opinion, a complaint to which Snapchat has largely failed to respond.
The company does not publish figures on the diversity of its personnel, pointing out its status as a private company.
On Thursday, Snapchat declined to discussing his staff's racial background, but according to a spokesperson, the company recently hired the recruiter to focus on under-represented populations and advance internal inclusion efforts.
For his part, Zhu said he'd keep his Snapchat account closed, adding: “I wonder if they actually need more users like us in order to say this isn’t okay.”
According to the Congressional panel, military officials misreproved ISIS intelligence
In Thursday's report, a congressional panell said the U.S. Armed Forces headquarters had edited intelligence reports to present a more optimistic image of the war in Iraq and Syria against ISIS than the events on the spot entitled.
The Interim Anniversary report of a working group set up by the Republican Chairman of the House Defence Forces Committee, the Intelligence Committee, and the Defense Appropriation Sub-Committee found ‘extensive dissatisfaction’ among intelligence analysts at the headquarters, who said supervisors tampered with their assessments of American efforts to defeat ISIS.
Headquarters, known as Centcom, is the headquarters of the Armed Forces' military headquarters in Tampa, Florida, which oversees U.S. Armed Forces operations in the Middle East and Central Asia.
“The intelligence outputs accepted by the higher-ranking Centcom executives typically gave a more positive picture of U.S. counter-terrorism efforts than the facts that happened on the ground justified. They were regularly more positive than the analyses produced by other factors in the intelligence community,” the report’s press release said.
“Centcom’s events are unacceptable – our fighters in the war suffer when bad analyses are presented to high-ranking decision-makers,” spokeswoman Ken Calvert said.
Centcom's management's failures go all the way to the top of the organization.
The ten-page report describes in detail the constant problems in headquarters descriptions and analyses of U.S. aspirations to train Iraqi troops in 2014 and 2015.
Although it doesn't provide clear evidence that the Obama administration's high-ranking regulators had ordered reports to be tampered with, it describes that analysts felt as if Centcom's executives had put pressure on them to describe the Islamic State's, ISIS-inflicted threat in a more positive light.
“For the entire first half of 2015, a number of press releases, statements, and testimonies to Congress were significantly more positive than the events themselves,” the report said.
For example, Centcom's authority publicly stated that a major offensive to retake Mosul could begin even as early as April or May 2015.
Mosul, Iraq's second-largest city, is still under ISIS.
“After months of the investigation, it’s very clear,” spokesman Mike Pompeo, a Kansas Republican, said in a statement.
From mid-2014 to mid-2015, senior intelligence directors of the U.S. Armed Force's Armedial Headquarters are manipulating headquarters intelligence outputs to belittle ISISI's threat in Iraq.
Republicans created a working group after hearing that analysts had raised concerns that intelligence about the Islamic state was being manipulated.
The report, which was published on Thursday, monitors more extensive results while the investigation is ongoing.
The Department of Defensive Superintendent has opened an additional ongoing investigation into Centcom's reconnaissance.
On Thursday, Democrat members of the House Intelligence Committee published their own findings, which were consistent with the conclusions of some Republican working group.
“Between 2014 and 2015, Centcom created an unnecessarily isolated process from the intelligence assessment of ISIS and Iraqi security forces,” spokesman Adam B. Schiff, the committee’s leading Democrat, said in a statement.
According to Schiff, this process “prolonged the release of intelligence outputs,” worsened the mood among analysts and “gave enough room for different perspectives.”
Schiff and the democrats said they did not find any evidence that the White House was trying to pressure Centcom’s analysts to reconcile their conclusions with a “predetermined or political narrative.”
The Daily Beast reported on Tuesday the preliminary results of the congressional investigation.
Last August, The New York Times reported that the Pentagon's superintendent has opened an investigation after Centcom's complaints from analysts.
Commander Patrick Evans, a spokesman for the Pentagon, said in a statement that the Department of Defense would not comment on the congressional report as long as an investigation by the superintendent is ongoing.
But he said, “experts sometimes disagree on the interpretation of complex information, and the intelligence community and the Department of Defense welcome a healthy dialogue on these essential national security issues.”
The study has other follow-ups than just what the question arises as to whether U.S.-led bombing operations in Iraq and Syria are successful (as a growing number of defense officials now claim).
Last year's accusations questioned how much President Obama can rely on headquarters to provide honest assessments of the war efforts of Iraq, Afghanistan, Libya and others.
Criticism of Islamic State intelligence reflects the controversy more than a decade ago when Centcom's intelligence analyst Gregory Hooker wrote an investigative article saying that, notwithstanding the protests of several analysts, President George W. Bush's regime advocated a small army in Iraq and spent very little time thinking about the consequences of the 2003 attack.
Hooker also played a key role in revolts on Islamic state intelligence.
According to the authorities, last year, the Hooker group concluded that, notwithstanding public statements to the contrary, airstrikes against refineries controlled by the Islamic State had not significantly weakened the terrorist group's finances because it had built temporary refineries to sell oil through the black market.
The Times reported in September that this discovery was not reported beyond headquarters.
Centcom's then commander, General Lloyd J. Austin III, was criticized last year after many legislators considered his testimony to the Senate to be an overpositive assessment of the war.
General Austin retired in April and was replaced by General Joseph L. Votel.
The United States wins for the fourth time in a scench in a women's field hockey, organizing a showdown with Britain
A day after three goals had been scored when the team defeated Japan 6-1, Katie Bam scored the U.S. women's field hockey team's first two goals on Thursday when the Americans defeated India 3-0.
Five goals in two games sounds like an unforgettable achievement, but 27-year-old Bam said he wasn't sure if he'd done the same before.
‘I don’t have a clue,’ he said.
“I’m not really the kind of person who keeps track of their goals.”
But that's what he knows: The Americans are in a 4-0 situation at the Olympic tournament and they share their group's first place with Britain, which was still unbeaten after defeating Japan 2-0 on Thursday.
Britain and the United States will meet on Saturday in the final game of the first round.
Both teams have made it to the quarter-finals, and this game determines who finishes first and who's second in their six-team group.
The first-place finisher avoids the strong Netherlands, the leader of the second group, a little longer at the start of the quarter-finals.
No matter how Saturday's game ends, the Americans have stirred with their surprising success here, four years after finishing last at the London Olympics with 12 teams.
Bam was on that team.
He grew up at Blue Bell, Pennsylvania, and picked up a field hockey bat for the first time at the age of 3 (his older twin sisters played for Drexel) to the national team at the age of 16, becoming its youngestest player of all time.
He wasn't at the 2008 Olympics because he didn't make it to the final player list.
In 2012, nothing went smoothly for him and his teammates.
The following year, Englishman Craig Parnham was appointed coach, and things began to change.
‘Cultural change, mental change, physical change,’ Bam said.
We didn't have the same pace of work in London as we have now.
We've made a turnaround.
On Thursday night, Bam started his first-half scoring with a backhand from close to the goal.
India had come into the game for a single win in the tournament, but the team was surprisingly tough and kept the situation 1-0 until the end of the third period, when Bam took over.
He carried the ball for a long time on the left side of the pitch, then twisted into the middle and pulled the ball into the goal, taking the Americans to a 2-0 lead.
Melissa Gonzalez added midway through the fourth period.
This shutout was the tournament's first U.S. goalie Jackie Briggs, who had conceded one goal in each of the first three wins.
He's in good shape.
So is Bam.
‘We’re in a good situation right now,’ Parnham said, but its goodness depends on the outcome of Saturday’s game.
Trampoline peace in the middle of the Olympic frenzy
Wherever you are, no matter what time it is, the Olympics are underway.
The events in Rio de Janeiro seem like a constant and inevitable message of furious competition and effort.
It's as if the Olympic torch itself is a flaming relay baton that's being transmitted from the archer to the swimmer and from the gymnast to the triathlonist.
It’s impossible to turn on a TV, read a newspaper, or listen to a neighbor’s conversation in the café’s queue, without hearing about Simone Biles, Katie Ledecky or the frenzy between Michael Phelps and Chad le Clos.
And then there's Logan Dooley.
Dooley, a first-timer of the Olympics and a future heartbreaker with his dimples, could very well be one of the names everyone knows, which are presented in the prime time and supported by sponsorship agreements and feel-good TV commercials - if he were a swimmer, a gymnast or even a fencer.
But Dooley, 28, from Lake Forest, California, competes for Olympic gold in the trampoline.
The Olympic flame does not illuminate all sports equally.
Further from the center, partly in the shade, are those species that are not universally accepted: air pistol shooting, archery, badminton, taekwondo.
We'll always have a breaststroke for a long time.
But if I can make a moderate number on my special favorite, I'd like to draw attention to the trampoline's discreet charm.
Although the trampoline has been an Olympic sport since 2000, when it made its first appearance at the Sydney Games, there is so little attention on its presence over the past 16 years that many I interviewed did not realize at all that it was an Olympic sport.
This can be regional ignorance.
Even though the United States has sent athletes to compete in the sport, it has never won a medal in it, and its athlete did not advance to the finals until 2012.
The Chinese and Russians usually distribute men's, Chinese and Canadian women's medals.
This year, Dong Dong of China and Rosie McLennan of Canada will defend their gold medals in London.
However, the trampoline is accompanied by a certain charm of the underdog, partly because it sounds more suitable for a clown school than for the Olympics.
In this I have to confess that I'm not an expert, I'm just an incapacitated fan.
There are opportunities for amusement in the sport, as you can see from the vibration of the endless bounces of drawing films, but it also has the gracefulness of gymnastics and diving, the two species that it basically combines.
The word ‘trampoline’ comes from the Spanish word ‘trampolín’, which means springboard.
On a giant trampoline, champions jump to a height of almost 30 feet, and they have to perform swirling movement sequences (turns, layouts and volts) and eventually land on their feet.
According to the official Olympic programme, they will be judged on the basis of implementation, difficulty and ‘flight time’.
There are 32 athletes from 17 countries, 16 men and 16 women competing in Rio.
The most common direction of the Olympics is straight ahead.
Those wishing for a medal have to go gold further, faster, up and above - sometimes to the finish line, sometimes back and forth all over the field.
They're hunting not only the finish line or the starting wall, but also, in televised news, another mockery boundary: the world or Olympic record.
This determined pursuit is noble, admirable and even fanatical - and it can even bring the viewer to tears.
But that's also the reason why watching the Games can also be a bit exhausting.
Trampoline offers a moment of rest here.
Trampoline direction is up.
The goal is not to move forward.
The competitor rises to a new ascent, appearing weightless like an astronaut, goes up, then down, then effortlessly up again.
Of course, there is no lack of competition or difficulty, but during the program there is no furious rush towards the end, no jumps to the distance, only the dreamy, smooth interruption of time and gravity.
It's as close to the Zen state as it can get at the Olympics.
The women's qualifying competitions and then the final will take place on Friday afternoon when the broadcast starts at 13 (ET), the men's competitions will also take place on Saturday at 13.
NBC will broadcast the finals as part of its afternoon repertoire starting at 14 o'clock both days.
In the midst of the Olympic rush, they provide a quiet, charming moment that is most likely not to be noticed in the general chaos.
And if consciousness is only gained by offering male athletes to stare and objectify (as the U.S. male gymnastics recently suggested, according to The Wall Street Journal), Dooley is just waiting to sigh at his new fan base.
‘Generation is protesting’ in Ethiopia, a long-term ally of the United States
Violent demonstrations in Ethiopia
Protesters demanding political change in Ethiopia have faced violent opposition from the government.
Eyewitnesses say dozens of demonstrators have been shot dead in clashes with police.
Is Ethiopia breaking down?
Over the past decade, it has been one of Africa's stablest countries, a strong ally of the West, whose economy is growing rapidly.
Over the past few months, however, anti-Government protests have shaken the country as they spread to more and more areas.
Last week alone, thousands of people rushed to the streets, demanding a fundamental political change.
According to human rights groups, the government's response was ruthless.
Eyewitnesses said dozens of unarmed demonstrators were shot and killed.
Circulated videos recorded in the protests, believed to be at the end of last year or the beginning of this year, show how security officials beat young people with sticks while they were forced to stand with their hands against the wall.
The United Nations' chief human rights authority is now calling for a comprehensive investigation.
‘Keeping this country together was always tricky, and in the future it will be even more difficult,’ said Rashid Abdi, project manager of the Horn of Africa in the International Crisis Group.
After Nigeria, Ethiopia is Africa's second most populous country, and Western countries appreciate its stability.
America's military and intelligence services work closely with Ethiopians in the fight against the region's terrorist threats, especially in Somalia, and hardly any African country receives so much Western aid.
Ethiopia's economy has grown at an impressive pace.
Its infrastructure has improved extremely much, and the capital Addis Ababa even has a new commuter train.
The streets of the city are typically quiet, safe and clean.
However, even though Ethiopia has not been a model of democracy. Human rights organisations have constantly talked about the oppression of the government, the country's opposition has been limited, and dissidents have been effectively silenced.
Many have been deported, detained, killed or driven far into the desert.
But this may be changing.
‘If you’re suffocating people, and they have no choice but to protest,’ Seyoum Teshome, a college teacher from Central Ethiopia, said.
The whole young people are demonstrating.
One generation is protesting.
Complaints are diverse and cover the choking grip of the power of the Coalition that dominates the land.
After last year's much-criticised elections, the ruling party and its allies took the seat of the last opposition, and now it holds all the seats in parliament.
At the same time, tensions on the border with Eritrea are increasing. The battle along this tense and controversial border required hundreds of lives in June.
Analysts fear that separatist groups such as Oromo Liberation Front or Ogade's National Liberation Front, which have been more or less defeated in recent years, may try to take advantage of the turmoil and re-arrange.
Numerous factors explain why, after years of turmoil under the surface, bitter emotions are now exploding.
The first seems seemingly harmless: smartphones.
Only in the last few years, a large number of Ethiopians have been able to communicate through social media, as cheaper smartphones have become more common and the internet service has improved.
Even if the government shuts down access to Facebook and Twitter, as it often does, especially during demonstrations, many people can still communicate through network proxies that hide location.
Many young Ethiopians said that this is how they gathered for demonstrations.
Secondly, Ethiopia's two largest ethnic groups, the Oromos and Amharas, get along better with each other.
Oromos and Amharas are not natural allies.
For a very long time, the Amharas, which come from Ethiopia's predominantly Christian highlands, prospered in politics and business, taking advantage of oromas, many of whom are Muslims and who live in the lowlands.
But this is also changing.
“We’re on our way to getting organized under one umbrella,” Mulatu Gemechu said.
The biggest demonstrations have been in the Amhara and Oramo regions.
Many Amharas and Oromos think that Ethiopia is unfairly dominated by members of the Tigre people. The people cover about six percent of the population and control the armed forces, intelligence services, commerce and politics.
The third cause of unrest is Meles Zenaw's loss.
Meles, a former rebel leader, was the prime minister of Ethiopia for 17 years until he died of a covert illness in 2012.
He was considered a tactical genius, a man who saw around the corners.
Analysts say he was particularly qualified to observe early signs of discontent and to use secret ambassadors to make opponents harmless.
‘Current government does not have such a basic instinct,’ conflict analyst Abdi said.
Ethiopia’s new prime minister, Hailemariam Desalegn, was picked up relatively unfamiliary to fill Meles’ boots.
Unlike Meles, which was from the tigre region of northern Ethiopia, Hailemariam is from the south.
Analysts say that security services controlled by Tigres don't trust him.
Many are afraid that this can lead to an increased bloodshed.
The last time Ethiopia was in turmoil like this was in 2005, when thousands protested for the elections, which, according to analysts, seemed like the government first squawked and then plundered them.
In the subsequent crackdown, many demonstrators died, albeit less than in the last few months, and that period of unrest passed relatively quickly.
Development experts have praised Ethiopia's leaders on visionary infrastructure planning, such as a new commuting train, and significant advances in the fight against poverty.
But clearly, this has not halted the intensification of contempt of the Ethiopian government within the country.
And now it's starting to take a dangerous, ethnic form.
Last month, protesters in Gondari, the city of Amhara, attacked attempts owned by tigre, and anti-tigre hatred is becoming increasingly common on social media.
Analysts say demonstrations put the United States and other West Allies in a clumsy position.
The American government has used Ethiopia as a base for drone flights to neighboring Somalia, although it recently said it had closed this base.
Even though Western countries clearly want to support democracy, they also don't want their already unstable allies to collapse.
‘This,’ Abdi said, ‘there’s a real balancing act.’
Donald Trump Explains Obama-Fortunized ISIS Claim ‘Sarcasm’
Donald J. Trump tried to stifle the dispute over his latest campaign on Friday, claiming that he wasn’t serious when, on numerous occasions this week, he insisted that President Obama and Hillary Clinton were “founders” of the Islamic state terrorist group.
The study raised new questions about Republican presidential candidate's ability to clearly communicate to the American audience.
It also placed his defenders, who have struggled to defend him during a turbulent period, into an inconvenient position in which they must explain remarks that he does not necessarily mean.
After an insinuation at Wednesday night's campaign meeting, Trump argued the same more and more eagerly in Thursday's interviews, in which he insisted that his intention was indeed to say that President and Clinton created ISIS.
However, in an early Friday morning Twitter post, Trump said that he was only sarcastic.
The release was a complete reversal from the previous day when Trump said in several interviews that he was indeed serious about the accusation.
When Conservative radio host Hugh Hewitt tried to help Trumk his remark by remindinging him that Obama wants to destroy ISIS
The latest storm of reviews comes in a situation where Trump has sought to refocus his campaign in the midst of the results of opinion polls falling into the economy.
Trump and Clinton fought to talk about the economy this week in Michigan.
On Friday, Trump again tried to return to the subject.
Escaping Twitter again, he warned that Clinton's proposals would lead to higher taxes, and that he considered the speech boring.
Trump has used sarcasm in the past.
Late in July, after facing widespread criticism, apparently suggesting that the Russians should hacker Clinton’s emails, he later explained his remarks by saying “Of course I’m sarcastic.”
The approach makes it possible for Trump to go at the borders of a political speech and then go back if necessary.
‘I think it’s a model where she tests certain messages that are important to her fans. She watches how they work and then ‘clarifies’ one way or the other, depending on the reaction,” said Ruth Sherman, a communications expert who has been following the language of both candidates.
But I also think he's cunning about his statements and knows that they're vague enough to be cropped through multiple lenses.
Rather than relieving his speeches for a great polling crowd, Trump has been even worse in recent days as his popularity has continued to decline.
A new NBC/Wall Street Journal/Marist survey published on Friday showed that Trump is lagging behind Clinton considerably in North Carolina, Virginia and Colorado, and that the race is tight in Florida.
As a rule, although Trump tends to avoid apologies for provocating things, he often accuses his critics of misunderstanding his words or misunderstanding his sense of humor.
As he faces an even closer look over the last few months of the presidential campaign, Trump's jokes have forced his loyal allies into an uncomfortable position where they have to publicly support him no matter how provocative his remarks are.
Former New York Mayor Rudolph W. Giuliani, one of Trump's most prominent supporters, went to a lot of trouble defending the candidate in an interview with CNN on Thursday.
“I believe there are real, political comments in his words,” Giuliani said when questioned of the veracity of Trump’s assertion that Obama and Clinton set up an Islamic state.
It's true in the sense that, before Obama, Isis was almost unknown, a small organization he called an amateur, completely wrong, and that's why this is what happened because he withdrew troops from Irach.
Most mainstream Republicans are trying to argue that Obama’s government, in which Clinton worked as secretary of state, allowed ISIS to get worse because it failed to effectively handle the situation in the Middle East.
Prior to Friday's meetings in Pennsylvania, Trump continued on Twitter to attack the news media, talking about his every word and being biased against him.
He mocked “bad, pathetic” television experts who are trying to find out about him saying “They won’t succeed!”
Some democrats mocked Trump's excuse as a new piece of evidence that he's unsuitable for the presidency.
Previously closed café returns to Greenpoint
Verb Cafe was a shabby, cash-approving place in Williamsburg that didn't have a wireless network, but which provided good coffee for 15 years.
Naturally, Bedford Avenue was too expensive for it.
The store that replaced the Verb in the summer of 2014, Soap Cherie sells cupcake-shaped soaps.
It was lamented by the locals as the most recent loss in a middle-class city.
Artist Molly Crabapple tweeted: “New York is change, I know, but I still can’t believe CBGB is dead and Chelsea Hotel is devastated and Verb is now a soap store.”
But if New York is a change, it's reinvention.
The revitalized Verb opened last November at Greenpoint, less than a mile from its original location.
The new Verb has Wi-Fi and you can pay with credit cards, but some of the staff is still the same and have the same unpretentious atmosphere.
The coffee's still good, too.
The new Verb will be led by Cisco Rodriguez, a long-time employee of the old café, who is the owner and president of the new location.
He started in Verb in 2001 and was there until the end.
When the client offered to fund him in a new place, he said he was thrilled.
Rodriguez, 36, kept a lot of things the same.
Verb uses the same bagel supplier and bought an espresso machine of the same shape.
It even used the lights of an old Verb.
But Rodriguez said he also wanted to try.
He brought new ingredients.
The menu includes bacon-egg-cheese sandwiches, and the coatings include, for example, capers and browned onions.
There's brown rice in the bowls.
You don't serve beer in the café anymore, but they make fresh juices.
Over the course of nine months, Rodriguez says he noticed a steady stream of old regular customers.
‘Williamsburg also became too expensive for many former customers,” he said.
One of the last Sunday, Rebecca Oliveira was one such customer.
Oliveira, 34, used to live in Williamsburg and regularly visited the Verb.
Even since he moved to Greenpoint, he wandered to the café.
He says that when Verb closed, he briefly gave up drinking coffee.
‘I realized that I enjoyed Verb’s coffee and culture,’ he said.
Now he's gone back to coffee, although on that day, instead of his regular iced coffee, he ordered a mixed chai drink, which is one of the new additions.
Another long-term customer, Tom Rosenthal, visited Verb faithfully since 2003.
He went there before work and always placed the same order.
“Every day fifteen past seven, regularly like a clock, I went in and they were preparing my spicy bagel with butter,” he said.
Photo. Rodriguez said he wanted to do experiments and bring new ingredients to the menu.
Today, Rosenthal is still making his usual order, but sometimes, due to the variety, he orders a spicy bagel with bacon, egg and cheese.
Rodriguez says he doesn't hold a grudge against a soap store that took over the old premises.
In fact, the owners of Soap Cheries offered to make a special soap for Verb, which would be used as an ingredient in the café's cold-extracted coffee.
‘I think we can still seize the offer,” Rodriguez said.
And Greenpoint's traditional Polish residents have also approved the café.
One of these new customers turned the menu into Polish, and Rodriguez said he was planning to put it on display at something to make his new neighbors feel welcome.
The new location has other advantages.
The local juicing site likes to bottle and sell cold brewed coffee.
It is possible that the café will expand into the adjacent building.
For many customers, it's enough for them to get their old favorites in the new Verb.
Joseph Whitt was a regular and said it was inconsolable when the place was closed.
When a new place was opened, Whitt said he felt like he had come home as he walked inside and ordered coffee.
‘I wanted to smell it,’ he said.
A Square Meal: the paralyzing food of the recession
In March 1933, shortly after becoming president, Franklin Delano Roosevelt sat down to eat lunch in his office.
President Roosevelt liked the refined Fifth Avenue dishes, such as foie gress pástee, and Maryland swamp turtle soup.
On that day, his diet was more modest: stuffed eggs in tomato sauce, potato purée and plum pudding for dessert.
“It was a demonstration of culinary solidarity for the people who suffered,” Jane Ziegelman said.
His spouse, Andrew Coe, added: “It was also a message for Americans about how to eat.”
The Brooklyn Heights couple are food historians.
Coe's latest book, Chop Suey, told about Chinese food in America, and Ziegelman talked about life from the Lower East Side apartment building through food in his book 97 Orchard.
Their latest collaboration, A Square Meal, which Harper publishes on Tuesday, is a history of American food during the Great Recession of the 1930's.
By showing some of their own culinary solidarity, they meet a reporter for dinner at Eisenberg’s Sandwich Shop, a tiny, unsophisticated lunch bar in the Flatiron area, which has been in operation since the stock exchange crash of 1929.
Ziegelman, 54, ordered a cream cheese-olive sandwich, and Coe, 57, took the dish with turkey, potato purée and vegetables.
When a reporter ordered meatloaf, the couple considered it appropriate to discuss eating the recession.
‘Loaves were very popular,’ Ziegelman said.
There was peanut loaf, liver loaf, bean loaf.
They were made from the ingredient and some cheap ingredient, which was used to extend the actual ingredient.
Imagine eating so many peanuts that they would form your dinner.
Coe grimaced.
‘It must’ve felt like lead in my stomach,’ he said.
Years before the recession, on American tables, especially in the countryside, it was like eating-as-much-as-you-can-pick-up table.
The women's groups cooked food for male farm workers, and fresh pie was served for breakfast, lunch and dinner.
A Square Meal chronicles the ways in which a nation survived the fact that it was suddenly no longer a country of abundance.
‘At that time, food became a central, distressing topic for Americans,” Coe said, explaining why he and his wife wanted to write about it.
Equally distressing was a new, inexpensive diet: Mystery dishes, such as loaves and drawers, became the main foods, and the box was a “great way to use food scraps,” mentioned Ziegelman, because chefs were able to hide the less appealing ingredients under a layer of creamy sauce.
Fresh fruits were replaced with cheaper dried fruits.
Muscle, which had been at the heart of American meals for years, became a sparingly dispensed luxury product.
Exceptional compilations were tested when the amount of calories and nutrient content were more important than the taste or even common sense in the kitchen.
While conducting the study, Ziegelman also prepared a dish of the era in which fried onions were filled with peanut butter.
“It wasn’t a popular addition to the dinner table,” Coe said.
Ziegelman said, ‘It was unreal.’
Peanut butter is by no means suitable for fried onions.
This was characterized by many household recipes.
Household experts such as Louise Stanley, director of the Federal Household Office from 1923 to 1943, guided the country's eating habits more than ever before or since.
Publishing recipes and articles in newspapers and magazines, they encouraged women to become ‘budgers’ and respond to the challenge of converting blobs like puréed spaghetti and carrots into tasty portions.
A Square Meal is a celebration of historical treats.
Particularly tasty are the reports of the government's responses to hungry citizens, as President Herbert Hoover's let me eat cake attitude.
At the same time, when he publicly praised his childhood for the simple, Iowa country food, he eates like the refined millionaire he had become.
‘He liked continental food, such as fish with cucumber sauce,’ Coe said.
He ate a tuxedo in the almost gilded dining room.
He was amazingly out of everything.
President Roosevelt could have made the same mistake if his wife, Eleanor, had encouraged him to be an example on his own stomach.
As stated in the book when Mrs. Roosevelt hired a housekeeper who had no interest in taste, the White House became a place that “not only served one of Washington’s drearies, but also the worst-prepared food.”
Many of the recessionary eating habits were abandoned as soon as the ground got back on its feet.
The legacy is our constant focus on calories and nutrition, Ziegelman said, and in the way science is applied to cooking.
The couple said that as they wrote the book, they gained a better understanding of Ziegelman's mother's eating habits, for example, about how she couldn't throw food away.
‘He was certainly afraid of wasting food,’ Coe said.
Ziegelman recalled, ‘He even made people sick.’
There's been a hat attack.
After the plates went empty in Eisenberg, he and Coe pulled out the dessert they had prepared at home: the plum dessert that President Roosevelt ate for lunch.
‘It’s called plum foam,’ Ziegelman said, adding that the overall trick was to give modest recipes a spectacular name.
The dessert did not have whipped cream, only plums, a little flour, sugar, water and cinnamon.
They all immersed their spoons unwillingly in a sticky, brown paste.
Surprisingly, it wasn't bad.
Coe sants once and again.
Sounded like a positive budgeter, he told his wife, ‘slightly chopped walnuts could help.’
Gilead will be compensated with legal fees for the hepatitis C patent battle against Merck
The U.S. District Judge has decreed that Gilead Sciences Inc is entitled to obtain the legal expenses it incurred in a hepatite C-patient lawsuit against the pharmaceutical manufacturer Merck & Co Inc.
In June, Gilead was released for US$200 million after they breached two Merck's patents related to Gilead's best-selling drugs Sovald and Harvon. This release tracked when an U.S. judge discovered Merck's misconductions, which included perjury and other unethical practices.
In Thursday's verdict, U.S. District Juror Beth Labson Freeman said Gilead is entitled to be exempted from the compensation it received in the event of opposition to the lawsuit.
Merck is trying to catch Gilead, which dominates the market with a new generation of hepatitis °C drugs, which can cure clearly more than 90 percent of patients with this liver disease.
The lawsuit dates back to 2013 when Gilead and Merck sued each other for ownership of the laboratory work on the basis of Gilead's drugs.
U.S.-backed forces say they've completely captured ISIS-ruled Manbij
U.S.-backed forces have completely seized the northern city of Manbij near the Turkish border after the last remaining ISIS fighters left the city, a spokesman for the group said on Friday.
Syria's Democratic Forces (SDF) are now investigating the city after the last group of fighters to hide in the city left.
More than 2,000 of the troops had been detained by fighters, Sharfan Darwish SDF allies, the Manbij Military Council, told Reuters.
“The city is now completely under our control, but we’ve started looking into it,” he told Reuters.
The Clintons made US$10.75 million in 2015, paid 34.2% in federal taxes
On Friday, Hillary Clinton released her tax return for 2015, which showed that the Democratic presidential nominee's and her spouse's income that year was US$10.75 million, and that their actual federal tax rate was 34.2 percent.
In 2015, the Clintons donated one million dollars to charities, mainly to the Clinton Foundation, former President Bill Clinton made nearly US$5.3 million in Speaker Reimbursements, and the Former Secretary of State announced that they had received 3 million from publisher Simon & Schuster for their book telling about their career at the State Department.
Clinton's vice-presidential nominee, Senator Tim Kaine of Virginia with his wive Anne Holton, posted 10-year tax returns.
Their actual federal tax rate in 2015 was 20.3 percent.
“Hillary Clinton and Tim Kaine set the benchmark for financial transparency,” Jennifer Palmieri, Clinton’s campaign assistant, said in a statement.
In a sharp contrast to this, Donald Trump is hiding behind false excuses and withdrawing his previous promises to publish his tax returns.
According to custom, U.S. presidential candidates will publish their tax returns, even though the law does not obligate it.
New York-Based businessman Trump and his lawyers have appealed the tax board's audit as a reason for refusing to file tax returns.
“It’s your turn,” Ian Sams, Clinton’s campaign assistant, said on Twitter when he posted a link to the Democrat Candidate’s tax returns.
On Thursday, Trump's Special Adviser, Michael Cohen, told CNN that he wouldn't allow Trump to release them until they're closed.
Trump's critics, including 2012 Republican presidential nominee Mitt Romney and other Republican colleagues, have said that his refusal raises questions about his capital, his donations to charity, his business dealings, and a number of other ties with Russia, for example.
Clinton has tackled the problem, releasing an online video on Friday highlighting prominent Republicans urging Trump to release their taxes.
On Thursday, he raised the issue in his economic speech in Michigan.
“He refuses to do what every presidential candidate of the past few decades has done: released their tax returns,” he told the public.
Politico has announced that Trump hasn't paid any or very few taxes in the 1990's, and the New York Times business column on Friday quoted numerous tax lawyers and accountants who said the situation could still be the same.
Federal tax rates have become a problem in the presidential election.
Clinton has backed a rule named after billionaire investor Warren Buffett, which would ensure those earning more than one million dollars a year would pay at least 30 percent in taxes.
President Barack Obama also supports the proposal.
At a Clinton meeting in Nebraska's Omaha last week, Buffett, whose Berkshire Hathaway conglomerate is located there, challenged Trump to meet and exchange tax returns.
Buffett said he is also undergoing an audit by the tax government, and that Trump is not “fearing” the tax collection agency, but about the voters.
Clinton's campaign has filed tax returns until 2007.
Today, the actual federal tax rate of the Clintons in Chappaqua, New York, was around 32 percent between 2007 and 2014, and their actual combined tax rate was about 40.5 percent.
Clinton's tax return for 2015 showed that only US$100 of their income came from wages, unlike the majority of Americans.
Their main investment was a low-cost, index-linked investment fund, and Clinton reported US$109,000 in dividend and interest income.
Complementary Reporting Kevin Drawbaugh, Edited by Jonathan Oatis
Chili powder condoms and firecrachers enhance Tanzania's elephant protection
Conservationists in Tanzania use an unusual remedy to prevent elephants from migrating into human habitats - they throw animals with condoms stuffed with chili powder.
The method has proven to be effective, and the Honeyguide Foundation, which came up several years ago together with Nature Conservancy in the United States, has intensified its advertising by training volunteers in the villages of northern Tanzania to use the non-violent four-step way to protect homes and crops without the animals getting injured.
In the past, many people used spears to protect themselves.
On Friday is World Elephant Day dedicated to the protection of these animals.
“Since we developed the tools, we’ve been observing a change of attitude in these communities, and they’re now much more confident that they can keep the elephants out of their fields without damaging them,” Honeyguide Executive Director Damian Bell said in a statement.
The first two steps include pointing the penetrating elephant with a flashlight and playing the horn.
If that doesn’t stop the animal, the so-called ‘chili cloud’ is thrown.
The condom is filled with a mixture of chili powder and soil, and then the condom is twisted so that only the fusing wire remains visible.
When the condom is lit, it erupts blashing and bursting chili powder dust into the air.
One smell is usually enough to send the elephant in the other direction.
The last resort is a trigger that shoots a Roman candle into the air. Fireworks produce a loud, bright explosion.
RT News: Jewish filmmaker criticizes Berlini hotel that removes Israeli area code at the request of Arabs
A French filmmaker with a Jewish background unleashed a media storm after unveiling that the Kempinski Bristol Hotel in Berlin does not list Israel’s area code. According to the employee, this is due to the ‘requests’ of Arab customers.
Claude Lanzmann, the author of a documentary about the Holocaust in Shoah, vented his frustration at the Berlin-based Kempinski Bristol Hotel, writing an open letter in a German FAZ publication.
During his recent overnight stay, Lanzmann tried to find an Israeli hotel in a list of destination codes.
However, the country was not mentioned among the countries to which you can call directly from the hotel room.
How can it be possible that in 2016 in Berlin, the capital of the new Germany, Israel has been removed and wiped?
Lanzmann wrote.
Kempinski Bristol, on the other hand, offers the opportunity to call Israel through its own call center.
In search of an explanation, the filmmaker raised the issue at the hotel reception.
Lanzmann wrote that the response from there “shocked him.”
The hotel’s prescription onist said it was “a premeditated decision by the management of the Kempinski hotels.”
The reason behind the decision caused even more dismay in the filmmaker.
“Most of our guests are Arabs, and they demand the removal of Israel’s area code,” the employee said.
Israel’s ambassador to Germany, Yakov Hadas-Handelsman, calls the case “a great injustice,” Zuddeutsche Zeitung reported.
“We were horrified and shocked by the incident.
It's a great injustice in itself.
But an even greater disgrace is the fact that this was the case in Germany and this particular hotel chain.
It doesn't need explanations.
“We expect the hotel to draw the right conclusions,” the official said.
The incident caused quite a reaction online when people called it a scandal.
Some argued that the Kempinski Hotel simply ‘gived way’ to the alleged demands of Arab customers.
‘Shame,’ said in another publication, pointing out that a hotel on the magnificent Kurfurstendamm street stands near a synagogue.
The hotel itself quickly denied all the charges and called the incident ‘insufficiency’ and “apologized” to Lanzmann.
‘A clear order not to include Israel in the list of shortcuts was never made,’ said Kempinski’s spokesman, according to Suddeutche Zeitung.
It adds that the area code has now been added.
Police used tear gas and rubber bullets against Brazilian students
On Thursday, students clashed with police in the Brazilian city of Sao Paulo when students came together to protest against the reforms of the government's public education system.
Police used tear gas and rubber bullets against demonstrators.
