RT Business: Russia-Iranian traffic is suddenly growing by 71%
After the lifting of the sanctions, the turnover of trade between Moscow and Tehran has increased by 70.9% compared to the previous year, according to Russia's ambassador to Iran, Levan Dzhagaryan.
Since international sanctions against Iran were lifted, Russian corporations have largely shown interest in doing business in the country, the ambassador said.
Russian companies will continue their existing ties with Iran, while new companies are ready to enter the market.
“Of course, one of the most important goals of Iran’s two-way cooperation is the expansion of trade,” Dzhagaryan said.
He pointed out that in the first five months of 2016, trade between Russia and Iran increased by 70.9 per cent compared to the same period in 2015 with a total of US$856 million.
Russia's exports to Iran, in particular, rost by 91.5 per cent, amounting to a total of 697 million dollars.
Russia's imports from Iran rose by 16 percent to US$158 million.
‘Trade growth was achieved through the supply of machinery, vehicles and weapons,’ the ambassador said.
In addition, Dzhagaryan is expecting a record number of Iranian tourists to Russia this year.
Last year, Russia's foreign council in Iran granted Iranian citizens about 35 000 visas mainly for travel.
For the first time, Iran was among the 20 countries with the highest number of tourists in Russia.
“It’s probably safe to say that last year’s record number of Iranian tourists in Russia are going to break,” he said.
At the same time, quite a few Russians are visiting Iran, which the ambassador hopes will change.
‘I can personally recommend a visit to the cities of Isfahan, Shirazi and Yazd, where monuments of ancient Persian civilization have been preserved in good condition,” he said.
Scotti Callum Skinner plunged to the finish line when the Brits grabbed their cycling gold
Scotland's Callum Skinner took glory in a men's team sprint at the Rio Cycling Stadium last nively when the British team's trio defensed their title from London in 2012.
The 23-year-old from Glasgow was anchored in a role in which his Scottish colleague Sir Chris Roy excelled, the legend was proud of the young man when he snatched to the finish line, snatching a victory from the prefabricated New Zealand team.
Britain won a shock four years ago, while Hoy won his fifth of his sixth Olympic gold medal.
And they repeated the stunt after a modest finish in sixth place at the March World Track Championship in London, even though Hoy has ended his racing career since.
The problem between Beijing and the Games in London was the replacement of Special Delegator Jamie Staff.
This was accomplished just in time when Hindes appeared.
Hoy's ending also created the void Kia's Emadi and Matt Crampton tried to fill.
Skinner was a man that British coaches held on to the Scot's potential that has now been realized.
Hindes, Kenny - who won the championship with Hoy four years ago - and Skinner set an Olympic record in qualifying, but New Zealand took the lead in the first round.
