The 23-year-old Glasgow-based Skinner said, “The comparison is flattering.”
I'm only here to do my best and gain a reputation.
In the final, we set an Olympic record and defeated the world champions.
It was absolutely incredible.
There's no better way to win.
After defeating Kenny and Hoy in London in 2012, Hindes admitted that he had deliberately crashed the track after a bad start.
He later withdrew his comments, and Britain's rivals have not complained.
This time there was no such problem with an overwhelming victory.
Hindes said: “It felt like being in London when there were so many British flags on the pedestal.”
As a team, we've found a connection in the last three months.
We've lost so many world championships, so it's simply great to win the Olympics again.
One event, one gold medal for the people, who won seven out of ten cycling sports both in Beijing and London.
If this reflects the development of the last five months, a successful five-day cycling stadium awaits us.
Based on Kenny's condition, it's not at all impossible to imagine him repeating Hoy's feat, winning three Olympic gold medals in one Olympics.
It would mean that Kennyn - who is exactly 12 years younger than Hoy, who was also born on March 23 (on that holy day in British sports, which is also the birthday of Steve Redgrave, Mo Farah and Roger Bannister) - the total number of medals would rise to six golds and one silver.
It'd be the same as Hoy.
Kenny will next compete in the sprint, which starts today and lasts for three days.
The 28-year-old from Bolton said: “I’m just enjoying this one.
The team competition is always the best.
We'll win it together with friends.
Winning Alone is, to be honest, lonely.”
Hindes said: “If he wins another two gold medals, he should be given the rank of knight.”
This was the only medal competition of the day, but Britain received more good news.
Sir Bradley Wiggins, Ed Clancy, Steven Burke and Owain Doull were the fastest in qualifying for a four-person, four-kilometer team pursuit.
Their time was 3:51:943.
The world record that won gold in London in 2012 is 3:51:659.
The first round and the final (22.42) will take place today, on the second day of the competition, when Wiggins is definitely the center of attention.
He had a controversy with the referees yesterday before qualifying, when officials invited him for a length measurement.
In a similar women's competition that continues and ends tomorrow, Laura Trott, Joanna Rowsell Shand, Elinor Barker and Milngavien Katie Archibald advanced at world record time as the clock stood at 4:13:260.
The British team has learned to trust that its mighty track cycling team will take over the middle part of the Olympics and achieve a whole of medals.
After presenting truly exceptional and complete control in Beijing and London, expectations have been downplayed before the Rio Games.
There has been drama in the background, the least of which have not been the separation of the technical director, Shane Sutton, in April due to sexism and temptation charges, as well as the end of the racing careers of majors such as Hoy and Victoria Pendleton.
However, based on the first-night races at the cycling stadium, it seems that Great Britain's now legendary fame rises to its peak at the perfect moment could once again be true.
Portland's smouldering future questioned for cutting an electricity contract
The future of Portland's Victoria aluminium-smelted, as well as hundreds of workers' jobs, is uncertain when the electricity company that distributes electricity to the factory revealed that one of the factory's owners is about to terminate the distribution agreement, so it's expected to cut all distribution agreements.
The measure follows the Victoria administration's decision earlier this year that the subsidies required to keep the factory operational will not be extended.
For a specified period of 20 years, the subsidy expires in November.
As many as 2000 jobs could be at risk at the Alcoan Portland factory.
As many as 2000 jobs could be at risk, directly or indirectly, due to the closure of the factory.
In addition, a snowball effect may be possible, as the smelter uses about 10 percent of Victoria's total electricity production.
In 2014, Alcoa closed Point Henry's moltard, which led to the closure of the Anglesea power plant last year.
Alcoan Portland's smelter loses money and is under threat of closure.
The measure now follows several years of speculation about the factory's future, which began as a result of a strong Australian dollar and has recently been due to the price of the factory's production.
AGL, which distributes electricity for a large aluminum smelter from its Loy Yang An power plant in Latrobe Valley, said it also expects three other owners to terminate their contract.
Most of the factory is owned by Alcoa and Alumina's consortium, and the Chinese CITIC and Japan's Marubeni Corp own the smaller shares.
“AGL is awaiting continued operations at the Portland plant, which means that the smoothy physical electricity demand will continue,” it said.
According to AGL, the agreements are so-called ‘protection agreements’, which were supposed to enter into force in November 2016.
The agreements have a 12-month notice period, so they will actually end in 2017.
“That’s why AGL expects the contracts to pay off in fiscal year 2017,” the electricity company said.
All possible profit effects for AGL from fiscal year 2018 onwards are currently limited by the strong outlook for the futures market for wholesale electricity prices.
AGL said it has already ‘anticipating termination’ reduced the value of contracts in its accounting by US$187 million.
Alcoa said the decision to end the agreements “won’t affect Alcoa’s ability to keep smelt in operation or secure smolten power supply.”
The U.S. company said it signed a 20-year deal with Loy Yang in 2010, and that the deal was supposed to come into effect in November this year, but it hasn't yet explained why it withdrew.
Analysts have been wary of the moltteress in Portland in Victoria, as well as due to the possible closure of the Tomago factory near Newcastle, because of the poor economic situation in the industry.
Earlier in the week, AGL's executive director Stephen Mikkelsen told analysts that Portland uses about 600 megawatts of electricity each year and Tomago about 900 megawatts.
‘If both factories exit the market, this is a significant reduction in market demand.
For us as an electricity producer, this wouldn’t be a good thing,” he said.
